regress/gate_arm.sh-831-# register's HOST bytes; the FAST path was always order-aware, so one STR wrote
regress/gate_arm.sh-832-# guest order to a warm page and host order to a cold one, and every strt (always
regress/gate_arm.sh-833-# general on its first access per page) was reversed.
regress/gate_arm.sh-834-#
regress/gate_arm.sh-835-# This probe runs BOTH byte orders. The six `be *` rows use -E barearm (BE) and
regress/gate_arm.sh:836:# are the DISCriminators; the six `le *` rows use -E testarm (LE) and are
regress/gate_arm.sh-837-# INVARIANCE CONTROLS -- on LE the broken code was accidentally right (host order
regress/gate_arm.sh-838-# == guest order), so they pin nothing about #372 and exist only to catch a fix
regress/gate_arm.sh-839-# that repairs BE by breaking LE. The `ldrb` witnesses are the purest form: a
regress/gate_arm.sh-840-# byte load has no byte order, so it exposes the raw memory layout directly, and
regress/gate_arm.sh-841-# the buggy column is the exact reverse of the arch column.
