.\_scratchpad\panel_tbi_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:997:Now Q4's row needs ASM=1 PTE construction — which requires the harness to know the ASM bit position; packet can't tell me (manual quote is ITBASM register, not PTE layout) — flag as the one fact needed to implement the Q4 row. Also, the expected-fail rows on the SHIPPED coarse build: TBIAP-on-ASM-page row asserts survival → FAILS on coarse build (TBIAP flushes all, including ASM=1 page) → must be carried as xfail/known-fail, which is exactly the "does not read as complete" mechanism: the artifact's ledger shows a standing red pair labeled "deferred ASM semantics," and a later partial implementation must turn exactly these green — with the TBIA companion staying green — and the pair's joint state distinguishes complete (both green: TBIA flushes ASM=1 page ✓, TBIAP spares it ✓) from partial/polarity-flipped (any other combination). Wait — check all combinations: coarse build: TBIA row ✓ green (flushes all incl. ASM page — correct), TBIAP row ✗ red (flushes ASM page — incomplete). Partial impl polarity-correct: both green. Polarity-flipped impl (TBIAP spares... flips: TBIAP flushes only-ASM?? weird): TBIA row red, TBIAP row red or... whatever — any deviation shows as red somewhere. And an impl that spares ASM on BOTH TBIA and TBIAP: TBIAP row green, TBIA row red → caught. And "partial" = e.g., implements sparing on TBIAP but TBIA keeps flushing all (which is CORRECT for TBIA!): both green → reads as complete — and IS complete w.r.t. these two assertions... but "partial ASM" could also mean: ASM entries match across ASN (the *reason* ASM exists) isn't implemented — i.e., an entry tagged ASM=1 doesn't actually match when the ASN changes. That's a swpctx/walk-level property, not a tbi property. Should Q4's row cover it? The packet's Q4 is scoped to the tbi fix: "so that a later partial ASM implementation does not read as complete" — I read "partial" as: implements the tbi-side sparing but not... hmm, or implements ASM tagging but with the TBIA/TBIAP distinction wrong. The two-row differential covers the tbi-side. The walk-side (ASM=1 entries match any ASN) is a further property (would need a swpctx+ASN-change row — but the packet says ASN is unread, so a coarse emulator passes an "ASM entries match across ASN change" row VACUOUSLY... wait, does it? If the cache ignores ASN entirely, then ALL entries match across ASN changes — so an ASM-match row would PASS on the coarse build (every translation survives swpctx) — vacuous pass, no discriminating power. So the only non-vacuous ASM observables are the tbi differential. Good — that reinforces: the two-row TBIA/TBIAP-on-ASM-page differential is the minimal non-vacuous assertion. I'll note the vacuity trap: any ASM row whose expected outcome is "entry survives" passes trivially on a cache with no discrimination at all (which is everything today), so the informative row must have a GREEN twin asserting flush-where-flush-is-due (the TBIA row). Nice — that's the crisp Q4 answer: the pair, one red one green on the shipped build; only the pair has discriminating power.)
.\_scratchpad\panel_tbi_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:1003:Also Q4 wording: "What should a regression row assert" — singular row. I argue ONE row is insufficient (the vacuity/green-twin argument); the minimum is the PAIR. I'll say: the packet's own question presupposes one row; the honest answer is that no single row suffices, and name the pair. That's a real answer, not dodging: a single TBIAP-spares-ASM row passes (vacuously or genuinely) in states we cannot distinguish... wait no: a single TBIAP-row is RED now and GREEN when ASM is implemented — it does distinguish now-vs-complete. What it misses: impls where TBIA wrongly spares ASM (TBIA row needed) AND — more importantly — if someone "fixes" the red row by making TBIAP a NO-OP or by sparing everything (TBIAP never flushes): the single row goes green (ASM page spared!) while TBIAP is now broken for non-ASM pages... BUT the EXISTING tbiap row (non-ASM test page expecting re-walk → 0xdddd) catches that: a no-op TBIAP leaves the old translation → got 0xcccc → FAIL. Oh nice — the existing witness already pins TBIAP-must-flush-non-ASM-page (the current tbiap row's test page presumably has ASM=0 → spared by a correct ASM impl? wait: correct ASM impl flushes non-ASM pages on TBIAP → current row still green ✓; a spare-everything impl leaves it → red ✓ caught). So combined: existing tbiap row (ASM=0 page must be flushed) + new TBIAP-on-ASM=1 row (must be spared) = TBIAP's ASM rule fully pinned, and the "spare everything" cheat is caught by the existing row. The remaining hole without a TBIA-twin: TBIA wrongly sparing ASM=1 pages. Is that plausible as a "partial ASM implementation"? Someone implements "skip ASM entries on invalidate-all-per-process"... TBIA is invalidate-all; sparing ASM on TBIA is a classic polarity confusion (TBIAP rule applied to TBIA). Plausible enough; the twin row is cheap (same harness page, call TBIA instead). So my answer: ship the pair; at minimum ship the TBIAP-spares-ASM=1 row as the standing red marker, and note the existing ASM=0 tbiap row already guards the flush-side. Good — this is precise and uses the packet's own rows as part of the design.
.\_scratchpad\panel_tbi_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:1073:Also — the packet says "Please do not simply ratify. Every fact in section 2 was corrected at least once..." — my audit did find genuine issues (the absence-inference hole; the selector-table unsettleable; the witness's over-invalidation blindness; the Q4 single-row insufficiency/vacuity point; the PTE-ASM-bit-position dependency for the Q4 row). That's a non-ratifying audit. Good.
