.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_053346\ollama_glm.txt:1:**VERDICT: The flagged premise is wrong, and refuting it needs no file and not even the unsettleable `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` fact — `IOC_CONF` accepts {0, 1} and `W_BASE0` accepts {0, 0x300800000}, so "exactly ONE value each" fails on two arms outright; only `W_MASK0` fits it. The store-before-check observation is READ-correct and is load-bearing: a "decline" that keeps the store is an accept with a complaint, so the fix must reorder to validate-then-store and leave the *previous* value. Q3: decline + latched announce is the only member the packet supports; plain ACCEPT hinges on whether window decode consumes these fields generically — and the packet contradicts itself on exactly that (Q2 says the fields feed decode; Q3's note says the emulator "does not otherwise model" them). Q4: the code's own wording supports "values developed against," not hardware legality, so a correctly-behaving guest can trip these — flagged as a reading, not a fact.**
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_053346\ollama_glm.txt:26:Which value instead: the **previous value**, not the reset value. Because: "decline" minimally means the write had no effect — leaving target state unchanged is what decline *is* [REASONED]; reset-revert is a second invented behavior stacked on an already-invented one; no hardware reverts a register on an unwanted write; and the reset values aren't in the packet at all, so "hold the reset value" cannot even be specified from this brief [READ: absence]. **Settling fact: the field initializers in the device setup.** How a reader knows: the accept-side row states the predicate (Q1), the latched announce names the rejected write, and unchanged-on-decline is the decline member's default; anything else must be documented as invented semantics.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_053346\ollama_glm.txt:36:- **decline + latched announce**: **the member I'd ship today**, with the Q2 reorder. Host stays alive, no guest-visible semantics invented, no new storage.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_053346\ollama_glm.txt:41:**Premise defect you should hear: Q2 and Q3 contradict each other.** Q2 asserts the fields "feed address-window decoding… may mis-decode later accesses rather than merely being inert." Q3's note asserts these are registers "whose values the emulator does not otherwise model." Both cannot be true as stated. If they're inert, Q2's mis-decode worry evaporates and plain ACCEPT is trivially safe; if they feed decode, Q3's note is false and ACCEPT requires generic decode. The field name `tlb_enable` [READ] is itself hard to square with "not otherwise modeled" — a translation gate nobody reads would be odd [REASONED]. **One fact settles all of this: every use of `window_base_0`, `window_mask_0`, `tlb_enable`, `ioc_conf` outside their write arms.** Until then, decline + latched announce; upgrade to plain ACCEPT (drop the guard, keep the store) only if that check comes back generic-and-safe.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_053346\ollama_deepseek.txt:1:VERDICT: The “exactly one value each” premise is false for three of four arms; the store-before-check order is a real defect that must be reversed; decline + latched announce is the best de-fatalisation; reachability by a correct guest is plausible but not provable from the packet.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_053346\ollama_deepseek.txt:18:REASONED: **Decline + latched announce** fits best. The code’s own wording (“TODO”, “value differs from the expected values”) indicates these are unimplemented or unexpected values, not necessarily illegal on real hardware. Faulting the guest would be wrong if real hardware accepts them. Deleting the check or using ACCEPT would still store the bad value, risking mis-decoding (Q2). Silent decline is acceptable but less useful for debugging. Decline + latched announce ignores the invalid write, preserves the previous known-good register value, and logs once — safe and informative.
.\_scratchpad\panel_sgirekills_pass4_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:1:**VERDICT: DO NOT CLOSE AS MEASURED.** The enumeration is well-formed and internally consistent; score arithmetic checks out; the self-check correctly repairs the vacuity. But the closing record is short on two named counts: (1) against the FINAL, self-checking row, exactly **2 of 11** aliasing mutants (bits 9, 10) have a logged kill — the other nine kills were measured against earlier versions of the very row that kills them, and this round's own doctrine (value-as-opcode scoring 46/46 while doing nothing) forbids carrying kills across a row edit by construction; (2) the premise "twelve bits in use, 0–11" is an assertion — if a bit ≥12 is in use, there is an unfired cell, which is precisely the bit-9/10 failure one level up. One command closes it: sweep `SGIRE_W_TEX_RD := 1<<i` for i ∈ 0..10 against `ae5c239`'s probe, expect eleven × 45/46 with only `tex xbit` red. Plus one grep for the used-bit set. Close after that, not before.
.\_scratchpad\panel_sgirekills_pass4_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:13:**Q5 — chain.** Closes logically; does not close as a measured record. What I swept from the packet: the eleven-cell mapping against the three inlined logs and every row definition; the kill mechanism per cell; the self-check's vacuity modes; session isolation; all in-diff correction notes against the rows they describe; the arithmetic (45/46 = xbit alone red; 44/46 = both L2 orderings red — all consistent). What settles closure: (a) the grep for the used-bit set; (b) one sweep — all eleven alias mutants against the single final probe, eleven × 45/46 expected. "Close every cell in ONE diff" deserves "kill every cell on ONE probe version." Denominator on record today: 2/11 measured against final, 9/11 by tight but unmeasured transfer.
.\_scratchpad\panel_sgirekills_pass4_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:10:**Q2 -- is the closure real?** Yes, within the class. A green BASE now proves its own setup fired (`1/11`), so the value-as-opcode vacuity is structurally excluded -- a silent setup failure reds the BASE (the packet's own contrast between the two 46/46s, READ). A single-bit mutant sets an earlier bit in the same session, suppressing the decline -> count 0 -> red; measured for five cells, mechanical for the rest (REASONED). One honest qualification: the self-check proves announce **presence**, not bit-**set**. For bits 6,7,9,10 the kill measurements close that gap retroactively -- a red IS suppression, and suppression requires the setup bit actually stored, so the kills double as bit-set proofs (REASONED). For bits 0-5 it rests on the idiom plus M10.
.\_scratchpad\panel_sgirekills_pass4_20260908\grok.txt:31:The old vacuity is gone. A setup that does nothing now scores `1/0` against want `1/11` and reds the BASE. `BASE_fixed 46/46` with that want is a measured proof that all eleven other announces were in **that** session, not a hope that the stores assembled.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_065643\kimi.txt:28:  The house rule as written in the #498 comment (`dev_sgi_re.c:76-86`) is **one bit per message site**. The catch-all is one site per direction with one text each → two bits. Per-*window* is inapplicable: the unmatched set is the complement of the windows, not enumerable as windows, and per-address is a table, not a bit. One bit would silence the second direction in the exact read+write probe pattern the comment describes, and `wr rd 2msg` pins that. The one thing the comment does **not** say: the latch is per-direction across all 2080 bytes, so a second *different* unmapped offset in the same direction is silent — `wr lat1`/`rd lat1` pin that deliberately (COUNT over two offsets), so it's an accepted, tested trade-off, but the #497 logic ("six windows walked in one session → six bits") applied with equal force would have argued for splitting the two unmatched *regions* (0x16e0 gap vs 0x1800 tail). Defensible as shipped; worth one clause in the comment if the file is next touched.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_065643\kimi.txt:37:  Assignment instead of accumulation, write arm (the read arm alone works too; both at once also works). Not among the nine built mutants — I read `killtable_20260831/mkvariant499.py` (base/fix/fix6/m1/m2/m3/m4/m5) and the artifact-era variants (`mwonly, mlatch, mnolatch, mret0, mshrink, mshrinkb, mtexshrink`): none touches the `|=`.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\ollama_minimax.txt:18:3. **Q1 tree precedent for what to do with unimplemented drawing ops.** The author explicitly says "grep the fork for an existing precedent before answering from first principles — the tree's own idioms are part of the design space." I cannot grep. Settle by: `grep -rn 'UNIMPLEMENTED\|unimplemented\|unknown op' src/devices/` and inspect each hit. The author's lean (decline + latched announce) is reasonable on first principles but the author is right that this fork's answer may already be shipping somewhere nearby. **Argument against the lean, in case it helps:** FAULT is reasonable if the fork already uses guest-bus-error to signal "the device rejected this write" elsewhere — that would be the precedent to find. Silent decline (with or without latch) is reasonable if the fork already no-ops unimplemented ops elsewhere. ACCEPT and BACKED ACCEPT do not apply here as the author notes; that part of the argument is correct from the packet.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\ollama_minimax.txt:37:The author says the reframe is the "entire warrant for treating this as a live functional defect rather than a robustness gap." That is correct on the *severity* axis but slightly understates the *robustness* axis: even after the reframe, the 252 undefined encodings still kill the host, and a guest that scribbles garbage into the op register (which MMIO devices routinely allow) reaches `exit(1)` just as easily as it reaches POINT. The fix should handle both — the documented primitives and the undefined encodings — and the option-set choice is the same for both. Worth naming so the fix doesn't accidentally treat "I added POINT and TRIANGLE cases" as sufficient.
