.\_scratchpad\panel_sgirekills_pass3_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:7:**DEFECT 2 (REASONED, answers Q3 vacuity).** The two rows assert `1/1` for `{TEX_RD_DECL, DECL_R}` in one session. They are vacuous under any mutant that does not collide on bits 8 or 11. Smallest edit that keeps the guard, keeps the rows green, and breaks the latch: change the mutant target from `1<<8` to `1<<9`. The `d->warned & SGIRE_W_TEX_RD` guard still exists; the two new rows still see bit 8 clear and bit 11 clear in every TEX session and every UNMAP session, so both literals fire once; but bit 9 is now double-used and silently suppresses whichever warning lives there. This is the same defect viewed from the mutant side: the rows do not test bit-independence, they test the one pair the panel happened to name.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\ollama_glm.txt:1:**VERDICT:** The 2-of-4 framing is arithmetically right as the packet states it — READ: mask `0xff000000` compares the top byte; the four constants' top bytes are 00/01/02/03; the switch implements 01 and 03, so POINT and TRIANGLE reach `fatal()`+`exit(1)` — but it *refines* the 254/256 census and must not silently retire it (both are true: 2 documented + 252 undefined of 256 masked values). Decline + latched announce is the correct option; FAULT has no in-packet warrant. Four load-bearing items are **not settleable from the packet** — the no-common-tail claim, the uninitialised-register⇒POINT premise, Q3's register trace, Q4's blast-radius completeness — each named below with the single fact that would settle it.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\ollama_glm.txt:17:## Q1 — decline + latched announce; caller does nothing
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\ollama_deepseek.txt:11:REASONED: Decline + latched announce is the best supported option from the packet. ACCEPT/BACKED ACCEPT are inapplicable because there is no correct value to compute. Silent decline or delete loses a real guest-behavior signal. FAULT is not justified by anything in the packet: no evidence of a guest-visible readback path or real-hardware fault behavior is provided. Single fact that would settle FAULT: whether real hardware raises a bus error on an unimplemented primitive or silently ignores it.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_060010\ollama_minimax.txt:1:**VERDICT: Decline the TEX read arm with a latched announce — not mirrored ACCEPT. Mirroring doubles the surface area of the index defect, returns known-wrong data the project's ethic forbids, and burns an extra oracle re-pin step. In the mirrored case the detector and the row's intent collapse to the same property, so no edit can break intent while passing detector — that structural fact IS the argument against mirroring, not just an argument for a comment.**
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_060010\ollama_minimax.txt:35:**Q3 — The third option is "decline + latched announce." Plus one thing I cannot settle from the packet.**
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_060010\ollama_minimax.txt:37:From the six-member set, **decline + latched announce** is the right member: the host stays up (the actual safety goal), the device returns no data (so it cannot lie), and the latched log gives the maintainer one signal that TEX reads are unsupported without spamming. FAULT is gratuitous — the brief's own framing of the row is bounds/correctness, not bus-error delivery. DELETE is the current fatal state, dressed up. BACKED ACCEPT is not distinguishable from ACCEPT for TEX unless "backed" means something the brief did not define (REASONED — the brief did not specify what "backed" adds for a 224-byte array whose storage already exists).
