.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\grok.txt:70:**Unmasking, not an option-set member:** the prologue already ran before `exit(1)`. At RESET, `drawmode == 0` takes two unlatched `printf`s (`!DE_DRAWMODE_PLANEMASK: TODO` and `not all DE_DRAWMODE_BYTEMASK set: TODO`). Today they print once and the host dies. After this fix they print on **every** start-flagged draw with default drawmode. Not this row’s to fix; it becomes live when this row succeeds.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_102152_500p2\ollama_glm.txt:9:Decline + latched announce is right. Delete/silent decline discard the only signal that a *documented* primitive went missing; FAULT invents an error response for a register write the real chip accepts; BACKED ACCEPT is incoherent (nothing to store). Against ACCEPT-POINT, your own Q5 finding is the strongest argument: POINT is the reset/unconfigured value, so ACCEPT converts "guest set START without configuring a primitive" from loud-once into a silent fg-colored pixel on every unconfigured trigger — you'd be accepting exactly the case you most want announced. ACCEPT also needs pattern/clip semantics right and pixel goldens no witness here provides, and TRIANGLE stays declined regardless, so the latch must exist anyway. ACCEPT POINT is a defensible fidelity *follow-up*, not a rider on a kill-removal diff. Note (READ from the diff): the latch is per-site, not per-value — a guest declining TRIANGLE then hitting POINT gets no second announce. Consistent with the project convention; worth knowing.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_060010\ollama_deepseek.txt:1:VERDICT: Do not mirror TEX reads. Decline TEX reads with a latched announce until the index fix lands; a mirrored ACCEPT is self-consistent only against a known-wrong store and makes the correct fix look like a regression.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_060010\ollama_deepseek.txt:11:**Q3 — From the six-member set, “decline + latched announce” fits better than plain decline or mirrored ACCEPT.**  
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_060010\ollama_deepseek.txt:12:READ: the six-member set includes delete / silent decline / decline + latched announce / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT.  
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_060010\ollama_deepseek.txt:13:REASONED: Decline + latched announce avoids host death, avoids returning wrong data, and avoids log spam. FAULT might be better if the guest can handle a bus error, but the packet does not say whether the guest/hardware would expect a fault; that is not settleable from the packet. BACKED ACCEPT would require either fixing the write arm or mirroring the wrong shift, so it is not a read-only third option. Delete is too destructive, and silent decline is worse than latched announce because it hides the unsupported read.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\codex.txt:1:VERDICT: CONFIRM — use decline + per-device-instance latched announce, keep `draw_primitive()` `void`, and let `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)` complete normally; several premises need precision corrections below.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\codex.txt:25:Decline + latched announce is the right member of the six-option set.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_102152_500p2\ollama_deepseek.txt:5:REASONED: Decline + latched announce is the right disposition for an unsupported guest op. ACCEPTing POINT would be feature work, not a bounds/correctness fix. The packet says the function has vertex registers decoded, but it does not establish that drawing a point is only a single pixel write; pattern, clipping, rasterization, and device-specific semantics could make it nontrivial. Silent decline would hide the event; FAULT would invent a bus error; delete would lose the diagnostic.  
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_081810\codex.txt:25:The visible new pair is not vacuous: one byte store feeds both predicates, and W/T move them differently. Exact non-vacuity of the four pre-existing component-2 row implementations cannot be re-audited from this hunk—their Python predicates are not included—but their reported per-row W failures are consistent with meaningful channels.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_102152_500p2\kimi.txt:15:  **Q1 — disposition: right.** ACCEPT-POINT fails on its own terms. The ACCEPT member of your set is "the model already computes the value" — it does not. Nothing in `draw_primitive` computes a degenerate point; LINE forces `linelen` to 1 (`src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:732-733`) but still runs the line stipple/ROP path, and the code itself notes direction flags are "presumably not for Points either" (`src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:701-702`). Writing new rasterization in a round whose purpose is to *bound* guest-reachable host behavior is scope inversion, and it buys nothing for TRIANGLE or the other 252 values — the default arm must exist regardless. The reset case cuts against rendering specifically: at reset a stray START-aliased access would silently poke a black pixel at (0,0) into tile memory for a guest that never meant to draw — silent state mutation versus a one-line announce. FAULT is wrong: the register write is legal and has *already landed* in `de_reg` before `draw_primitive` is called (`src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1189-1190`); a bus error would misreport a modeling gap as a guest error. Decline + latched announce stands, and if a later round ACCEPTs POINT the arm and bit stay load-bearing.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_102152_500p2\codex.txt:5:Decline + latched announce is the right hardening disposition.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_102152_500p2\codex.txt:52:Dropping the `!` is not an escape from a correctly implemented “exactly one site-unique message” row: it produces zero messages from the zeroed latch. It only escapes a static guard-presence check or a loose “at most once” assertion.
.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_102152_500p2\grok.txt:9:Decline + latched announce is the correct member of the six. The case for drawing a point from the already-decoded vertices is the one that looks tempting and is wrong.
