.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_094355_texguard\ollama_glm.txt:1:{"model": "glm-5.3", "created_at": "2026-08-31T14:49:56.599175484Z", "response": "", "thinking": "Let me carefully parse this brief.\n\nWe have a packet-fed seat asked to review a bug fix decision. The context: GXemul 0.7.0 hardened fork, file `dev_sgi_re.c`, `CRIME_RE_TEX` write arm. The code:\n\n```c\n} else if (relative_addr >= CRIME_RE_TEX && relative_addr < CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0) {\n        if (len != 8) {\n                ... decline, latched ...\n        } else if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {\n                int tlbi = ((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff;\n                for (size_t hwi = 0; hwi < len; hwi += sizeof(uint16_t)) {\n                        d->re_tex[tlbi] = data[hwi]*256 + data[hwi+1];\n                        tlbi++;\n                }\n        } else { fatal(\"TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TEX\\n\"); exit(1); }\n}\n```\n\nWait \u2014 interesting. Note the index computation: `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`. The window is `CRIME_RE_TEX` to `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` (224 bytes = 112 halfwords). The `& 0xff` masks to 8 bits... Hmm, but if relative_addr is relative to the device base, `relative_addr & 0xff` \u2014 that's suspicious too. Actually wait, we need to think about what `relative_addr` is. The window check is `relative_addr >= CRIME_RE_TEX && relative_addr < CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0`. If CRIME_RE_TEX is some offset within the device, then `relative_addr & 0xff` doesn't subtract the base. E.g., if CRIME_RE_TEX = 0x2000 (say), then relative_addr in [0x2000, 0x20e0), and `relative_addr & 0xff` gives values in [0x00, 0xe0). Hmm, that only works if CRIME_RE_TEX is 256-aligned. Actually the `& 0xff` after `>> 3` then `& 0xff` again... The final `& 0xff` masks the index to 0..255, but the array is 112 entries. With `>> 3`, max index before mask: (0xe0-1)>>3 = 0xdf>>3 = 27. Then loop adds 3 more (4 halfwords written: hwi=0,2,4,6 \u2192 tlbi = base, base+1, base+2, base+3). So indices 27..30. That matches \"indices 27..30 at every in-window offset\" \u2014 READ.\n\nWith `>> 1`: (0xdf) >> 1 = 111 (0xdf = 223, 223>>1 = 111). Then indices 111..114 \u2014 114 is out of bounds for re_tex[112] (valid 0..111). So writes to re_tex[112], [113], [114]? Wait: tlbi starts at 111 for offset 0xdf, writes 111, 112, 113, 114. Index 111 is valid (last element), 112-114 overflow into `re_linear_a[32]` (uint32_t array following). The brief says \"at offset 0xdf it writes halfword indices 111,112,113,114\" \u2014 READ. Yes.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 with 8-byte writes at unaligned offset 0xdf: the byte offset 0xdf within the window; but relative_addr & 0xff \u2014 the window presumably starts at a 256-byte aligned base? Actually the brief says the window is 0xe0 = 224 bytes. If CRIME_RE_TEX is, say, 0x1000, then relative_addr & 0xff for addresses 0x1000..0x10df gives 0x00..0xdf. OK.\n\nAlso note: the loop writes halfwords from `data[hwi]*256 + data[hwi+1]` \u2014 big-endian composition, 4 halfwords for len=8. So tlbi increments 4 times total: base..base+3.\n\nPremise 2 says \">> 3 is bounds-safe UNCONDITIONALLY \u2014 indices 27..30 at every in-window offset.\" Check: max in-window byte offset is 0xe0 - 1 = 0xdf = 223. But wait, len==8 stores 8 bytes starting at relative_addr; the last byte accessed is at relative_addr+7, which could be 0xdf+7 \u2014 beyond the window end but the window check only checks the start address. Hmm, that's a separate issue: the store of 8 bytes at offset 0xd8..0xdf is the last fully-in-window; at offset 0xda, bytes go to 0xe1, spilling past the 0xe0 window into... the next register region. But that's about bytes read from `data`, not array indices. Actually the loop condition is `hwi < len`, so it always writes 4 halfwords regardless. The index overflow at 0xdf: base = 223>>3 = 27, writes 27,28,29,30. Array is 112. Fine.\n\nWith >>1: base = 111, writes 111,112,113,114. 112..114 OOB. But premise 3: MIPS guest paths can't deliver unaligned len==8 (ld/sd raise address-error exceptions; ldl/ldr/sdl/sdr mask). Premise 4: debugger `put d` can, prints a warning but proceeds. Premise 5: re_tex has no reader \u2014 severity nil.\n\nPositions:\nA: shift `>>3` \u2192 `>>1` PLUS local guard `if (len != 8 || (relative_addr & 7))`.\nB: shift alone with comment.\nC: ship nothing.\n\nThe in-tree fact: two LINEAR arms have ~30-line comment from a prior round: guest-path invariant stated for guest paths; `put d` named as the ONE exception, excluded as \"laundering rather than witnessing\"; the over-run stays FILED (as `sgilinearoob`), not guarded.\n\nSo prior precedent in the same file: document-and-file rather than add-guard, for an over-run of the same class that exists today.\n\nQ1: which ships? The author moved from A to B based on the precedent. Q2: disanalogy \u2014 LINEAR over-run exists today; TEX over-run would be newly introduced by the fix. Is creating a member of a filed defect class categorically different from inheriting one? Q3: does the row ship at all \u2014 C says no, but a prior ruling made this row a PREREQUISITE of a HIGH row (sgirekills creates re_tex's first reader). Tension between \"do not ship\" and \"runs first\". Q4: smallest edit that reverts the shift and still passes a detector row that stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08 and greps array name and index. Under A, the store is aligned so deleting the alignment half of the guard escapes the detector. Does B have an analogous hole?\n\nWait, Q4 is subtle. The detector row: stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08 (aligned) and greps the array name and index. So the detector presumably checks that the code indexes re_tex with the correct index for offset 0x08: under >>3, offset 0x08 \u2192 index 1; under >>1 \u2192 index 4. The detector presumably greps for `>> 1` or checks the computed index? \"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 probably a source grep for `re_tex[` with some index expression, or a runtime check that the value lands at the right place.\n\nQ4 asks: what's the smallest edit that reverts the shift (i.e., undoes the fix, going back to >>3) and still passes the detector row? That's an adversarial question: how can a lazy/future editor revert the fix while the detector still passes? Under A, the store at +0x08 is aligned, so deleting the alignment conjunct `(relative_addr & 7)` from the guard escapes the detector \u2014 one seat already found that. Does B have an analogous hole?\n\nHmm wait. Let me re-read: \"What is the smallest edit that reverts the shift and still passes a detector row that stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08 and greps the array name and index? That row is the natural anchored oracle. Under position A it is aligned, so deleting the alignment half of the guard escapes it \u2014 one seat already found that. Does position B have an analogous hole?\"\n\nSo the detector row is an oracle test: it stores 8 bytes at +0x08 via... presumably a guest path or debugger, and checks that the data lands in re_tex at the right indices (4 and 5? or greps the source for the index expression). The question is about revert-resistance: if someone later reverts the shift, does the detector catch it?\n\nUnder A (shift + guard), the guard has two conjuncts: `len != 8` and `(relative_addr & 7)`. The detector stores at +0x08, aligned, len 8 \u2014 passes the guard. If someone reverts the shift (>>1 \u2192 >>3), the detector should catch it IF it checks where the data lands (index 4 vs 1). But if the detector only greps the array name and index \u2014 hmm, \"greps the array name and index\" might mean it greps the source code for `re_tex[tlbi]` and the index expression? Actually maybe the detector is a source-grep detector: grep for `re_tex[` and check the index expression contains `>> 1`. Then \"smallest edit that reverts the shift and still passes\" \u2014 e.g., change `>> 1` to `>> 3` in a way the grep still matches? Like `(relative_addr & 0xff) >> 1` vs... hmm.\n\nActually, I think the detector row is a behavioral test: store 8 bytes at +0x08 via the guest, then check (via debug output? or via a subsequent read? but there's no reader...) Hmm, but re_tex has no reader except a debug() on the next line. The debug() prints the stored value. So the detector could store and then grep the debug log for the value at the index. \"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 maybe it greps the debug output which contains `re_tex[N] = value`.\n\nUnder >>3 at +0x08: tlbi = 8>>3 = 1, writes indices 1..4. Under >>1: tlbi = 4, writes 4..7. So a detector storing a distinctive pattern at +0x08 and grepping for `re_tex[4]` (or the value at index 4) distinguishes.\n\nNow Q4: \"smallest edit that reverts the shift and still passes\" \u2014 i.e., a future editor wants to undo the fix (or accidentally does), what's the smallest edit that evades the detector? Under A: delete the alignment conjunct from the guard \u2014 the detector's store is aligned so it still proceeds, and... wait, no. Deleting the alignment half of the guard doesn't revert the shift. Hmm, let me re-read.\n\n\"Under position A it is aligned, so deleting the alignment half of the guard escapes it \u2014 one seat already found that.\"\n\nHmm. So under A, the guard is `if (len != 8 || (relative_addr & 7))` decline. The detector stores at +0x08 \u2014 aligned, len 8 \u2014 so the store proceeds. Now, if a future edit deletes the alignment half (making the guard just `len != 8`), the detector still passes (store still proceeds, index still correct). But that doesn't revert the shift... So what does \"escapes it\" mean? Maybe the concern is different: the guard is a piece of the fix; deleting the alignment conjunct removes the protection against the OOB, and the detector doesn't notice because its store is aligned. So the detector row does not anchor the guard \u2014 only the shift. The guard is un-anchored, deletable without detection. That's a hole in A's detector coverage: A adds the guard, but the natural oracle (store at aligned +0x08) cannot witness the guard, so the guard can be silently deleted later.\n\nSo Q4 is: the detector row (store 8 bytes at aligned +0x08, grep array name and index) anchors the shift. Under A, the alignment conjunct of the guard is invisible to this detector (aligned store never trips it), so deleting it escapes detection \u2014 a hole. Does B have an analogous hole? Under B (shift alone, comment), the detector anchors the shift: reverting >>1 \u2192 >>3 changes where the data lands at +0x08 (index 1 vs 4), so the grep of array name and index catches it. Unless... the detector greps the source? Hmm.\n\nWait, \"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 maybe the detector greps the SOURCE for `re_tex` and the index expression. If the detector greps source for `>> 1` near `re_tex[`, then a revert to `>> 3` fails the grep. But an edit like changing `>> 1` to `/ 2`? That's equivalent to >>1 for non-negative \u2014 no wait, /2 == >>1 for unsigned. So `/ 2` reverts... no, /2 IS the shift semantically. Hmm, \"reverts the shift\" means going back to >>3 (the bug). Smallest edit that reverts to the buggy behavior and passes the grep... If grep looks for `>> 1`, you could write `>> 3 + 2`? No...\n\nAlternatively the detector is behavioral: store at +0x08, then read back or check debug output for index 4. Smallest revert that passes: change the store loop to write at `tlbi = base*4`? Hmm: `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) * 4`? At +0x08: 8>>3 = 1, *4 = 4 \u2014 same as >>1 at that offset! So `>> 3` then `*4` passes a detector that only tests +0x08 but reverts the behavior at other offsets? No wait \u2014 at +0x08, (8>>3)*4 = 4 = 8>>1. But at +0x10: (16>>3)*4 = 8 = 16>>1. Actually (x>>3)*4 = x>>1 for x multiple of 8 \u2014 wait, (x>>3)*4 = (x/8)*4 = x/2 = x>>1 exactly when x is a multiple of... no: (x>>3)*4 = 4*floor(x/8). x>>1 = floor(x/2). For x=8: 4 vs 4. x=12: 4 vs 6. So for 8-aligned offsets they agree! Interesting: for 8-aligned offsets, (x>>3)*4 == x>>1. Hmm wait, that means at aligned offsets, `>>3` with... no. Hold on.\n\nActually wait \u2014 that's a mathematical curiosity: at 8-aligned offsets, floor(x/8)*4 = x/2? x = 8k: floor(8k/8)*4 = 4k; x>>1 = 4k. Yes! Equal. So a detector that only stores at 8-ALIGNED offsets cannot distinguish `>>1` from `>>3` followed by `*4`? But `>>3` alone (without *4) gives 8k>>3 = k, whereas >>1 gives 4k. Different. OK.\n\nHmm, but there's an even more interesting hole: does the detector distinguish >>3 from >>1 at +0x08 at all? +0x08: >>3 \u2192 index 1; >>1 \u2192 index 4. Yes, distinguishable if it checks the index. Good.\n\nBut here's a subtle question: is +0x08 in-window and aligned \u2014 yes. Under the CURRENT code (>>3), a store at +0x08 writes indices 1..4. Under the fix (>>1), indices 4..7. Note the overlap at index 4! If the detector greps for the array name and index \u2014 say it checks `re_tex[4]` got the LAST halfword under >>3 (indices 1,2,3,4 \u2014 index 4 gets the 4th halfword) vs under >>1 index 4 gets the FIRST halfword. So if the detector checks index 4 contains the first data halfword, >>3 fails (it would contain the fourth). If it checks index 4 contains the fourth halfword, >>1 fails. The overlap means the detector must check the value-index pairing, not just \"index 4 was touched\". Hmm, \"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 the debug() on the next line presumably prints something like `re_tex[%i] = 0x...`. So the detector greps the log for the exact line `re_tex[4] = <first halfword>`. OK.\n\nNow: what's the smallest edit that reverts the shift and still passes? Under B (shift alone): you'd need to make +0x08 store land at index 4..7 while reverting elsewhere. E.g., `int tlbi = ((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff; ... d->re_tex[tlbi] ... tlbi++;` \u2192 revert to `>> 3` but change `tlbi++` to `tlbi += 4`? At +0x08: base 1, writes indices 1, 5, 9, 13 \u2014 no. Hmm.\n\nOr: `tlbi = ((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 1) & 0xff` \u2192 revert to `>> 3` and multiply: `tlbi = (((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) << 2)`. At aligned offsets identical to >>1; at unaligned offsets different (and OOB differently). If the detector only tests +0x08 (aligned), this edit passes. So B's hole: the detector's single aligned offset can't distinguish `>>1` from `>>3<<2` (equivalently `(x>>3)*4`), which agrees with >>1 on all 8-aligned offsets but reverts... wait, does it revert? (x>>3)*4 at aligned x equals x>>1 \u2014 so on the aligned subset it's NOT a revert at all; it's semantically identical wherever the guard/invariant restricts to aligned. Hmm! Interesting. Under B, with no guard, unaligned stores via `put d` would give (x>>3)*4: at 0xdf: 27*4=108, writes 108..111 \u2014 in bounds! Actually (x>>3)*4 max = 27*4 = 108, +3 = 111 \u2014 always in bounds! Ha. That's a \"revert\" that is bounds-safe at every offset and index-correct at every 8-aligned offset. It only mis-indexes for unaligned offsets (which guest paths can't produce). So actually that edit is arguably BETTER than plain >>1 \u2014 it's bounds-safe unconditionally AND correct on the guest-reachable set. But it's a weird way to write it.\n\nHmm wait, is that right? (x>>3)*4: x in [0, 0xdf]. x>>3 in [0,27]. *4 \u2192 [0,108]. Writes 108..111. In bounds (112 elements: 0..111). Yes! Bounds-safe unconditionally. And for 8-aligned x, equals x>>1 \u2014 index-correct. For unaligned x (only reachable via put d), it rounds DOWN to the containing 8-byte block's first halfword index... wait no: (x>>3)*4 vs x>>1: x=0xda (unaligned): (0xda>>3)*4 = 26*4 = 104; 0xda>>1 = 109. So it indexes 104 instead of 109. Not OOB, just \"wrong\" indexing for a case that can't happen via guest and that has no reader anyway.\n\nOK so this is a genuine finding for Q4: under B, the detector anchored at a single aligned offset +0x08 cannot distinguish `>> 1` from `>> 3` composed with `* 4` (or `<< 2`), because the two agree on every 8-aligned offset. But \u2014 and here's the twist \u2014 that \"revert\" is not a revert of the safety property; it's only a revert of the textual form. Actually is it a revert of correctness? For all guest-reachable (aligned) stores it's identical. So semantically, on the reachable set, it's not a revert at all. The detector hole exists but the escaping edit is behavior-preserving on the reachable set. Whereas under A, the escaping edit (deleting the alignment conjunct) REMOVES protection: it re-opens the OOB for `put d` unaligned stores... wait no. Under A, deleting the alignment conjunct means unaligned len==8 stores proceed \u2192 with >>1, offset 0xdf \u2192 indices 111..114 \u2192 OOB into re_linear_a. So the escaping edit under A restores an actual OOB (the exact defect the conjunct was added to prevent) and the detector can't see it because the detector's store is aligned. That's a REAL hole: the anchored oracle actively fails to protect the guard.\n\nUnder B, the analogous escaping edit... what would it be? Under B there's no guard, so the \"smallest edit that reverts the shift\" is `>> 1` \u2192 `>> 3`, and the detector at +0x08 catches it (index 1 vs 4 \u2014 as long as the grep checks value-at-index, not just that some index was written; note the overlap at index 4! If the detector merely greps for `re_tex[4]` appearing in the log, BOTH >>3 (writes 1,2,3,4) and >>1 (writes 4,5,6,7) produce a `re_tex[4]` line! So a naive grep for \"array name and index\" \u2014 `re_tex[4]` \u2014 PASSES under both! Whoa. That's a hole in the detector itself, and it's shared by A and B.)\n\nLet me check: under >>3 at +0x08, the four halfwords go to indices 1,2,3,4. The debug() on the next line \u2014 presumably prints each stored entry or the array. If the detector greps for `re_tex[4]` (name and index), >>3 writes index 4 (with the FOURTH halfword), >>1 writes index 4 (with the FIRST halfword). A grep that doesn't check the VALUE passes both. A grep that checks `re_tex[4] = <data halfword 0>` catches the revert. So the detector's discriminating power depends on whether it greps value+index or just index. The brief says \"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 not the value! Hmm, \"stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08 and greps the array name and index\". If it greps name and index only, then at +0x08: >>3 \u2192 indices 1..4 (contains 4); >>1 \u2192 indices 4..7 (contains 4). Both contain index 4! So the detector as described does NOT discriminate the shift at +0x08 unless it also checks the value or greps for a non-overlapping index like 5, 6, or 7 (>>1 writes 5,6,7; >>3 doesn't). Grep for `re_tex[5]` would discriminate. But \"the array name and index\" (singular \"index\") suggests one index. If that index is 4 \u2014 the overlap \u2014 the detector is vacuous: it passes the ORIGINAL code too, meaning even the full revert `>>1 \u2192 >>3` passes it. Hmm, but the brief says the detector is meant to be the \"natural anchored oracle\" for the shift. If the natural anchor is index 4, it's broken by the overlap. If it's index 5/6/7 or a value check, it works.\n\nActually wait \u2014 maybe the intended grep is for the index EXPRESSION in the source, not the log. \"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 e.g., grep the source for `re_tex[tlbi]` and the `>> 1` index computation? Hmm, \"stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08 and greps the array name and index\" \u2014 the row does two things: (1) a store (behavioral), (2) a grep (source-level?) of array name and index. Maybe the grep is over the source: check that the source contains `re_tex` indexed via `>> 1`? If the grep is for the literal text `>> 1`, then an escaping edit under B: change `>> 1` to `>> 3` \u2014 fails grep. Change to `/ 2` \u2014 passes grep? If grep looks for `>> 1` literally, `/ 2` fails the grep (grep wouldn't match), so that edit fails the detector too (the detector fails when the fix is semantically present but textually absent \u2014 false negative, annoying but not a hole in the revert direction). To revert AND pass: need text matching `>> 1` but semantics of `>> 3`. E.g., `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) >> ... ` hmm. `x >> 1` must appear as a substring while the actual index is x>>3. E.g., write `tlbi = ((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3); /* was >> 1 */` \u2014 a comment! If the grep is textual and matches comments, adding `>> 1` in a comment while code says `>> 3` passes the grep. That's the classic textual-grep hole. Under B, the comment is REQUIRED (B ships \"the shift alone, with a comment\") \u2014 if the comment mentions the old `>> 3`... hmm, or the fix comment mentions `>> 1`. A future revert that flips the code but leaves the comment passes a textual grep.\n\nBut actually I shouldn't over-engineer; the brief says the detector row \"stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08 and greps the array name and index\" \u2014 I think the most natural reading: the row performs a store via the debugger or guest at +0x08, then greps the resulting debug/log output for the array name and the index at which the data landed. Given the debug() on the next line prints the store. The phrase \"That row is the natural anchored oracle\" \u2014 it anchors the fix behaviorally.\n\nGiven ambiguity, I should flag: the packet does not settle whether the grep includes the VALUE or only the index, and whether it greps log output or source text. And the overlap at index 4 matters: at +0x08, buggy >>3 writes indices 1-4 and fixed >>1 writes 4-7; they share index 4. If the oracle greps index 4 without value, it passes both the fix and the full revert \u2014 the oracle is vacuous, and BOTH A and B inherit the hole. If it greps a value or an index in {5,6,7}, it discriminates.\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me reconsider. Maybe \"greps the array name and index\" refers to grepping the source for the array name `re_tex` and the index expression \u2014 i.e., the detector row greps the PATCH/source for `re_tex` and `>> 1` (the index computation). \"stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08\" \u2014 the behavioral part. Both parts together: behavioral store confirms data lands correctly, source grep confirms the expression. Under A, deleting the alignment conjunct of the guard: the behavioral part still passes (aligned store proceeds, correct index), and the source grep (array name + index shift) still passes (the shift is still there). So the guard's alignment conjunct is un-anchored \u2014 deletable without tripping the detector. That matches \"deleting the alignment half of the guard escapes it\". Yes \u2014 so the detector anchors: (a) the store works at +0x08 (behavioral), (b) the array name and index expression in source (grep). The guard is not covered by either. Good, that reading is consistent.\n\nUnder B: is there an analogous hole? B ships only the shift + comment. The detector anchors the shift behaviorally (store at +0x08 lands at indices 4..7 \u2014 wait, does the behavioral check verify indices? If the behavioral part stores 8 bytes and the only observable is the debug() print... The detector \"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 from the debug log, the entries printed are re_tex[4..7] with values. Under revert to >>3, the log shows re_tex[1..4]. A grep for the array name and index \u2014 if it greps for index 4, both pass (overlap!). If it greps for index 7 (or 5), only the fix passes.\n\nHmm, I keep going back and forth. Let me just be honest about the ambiguity and analyze both readings, since the packet genuinely doesn't settle it. Actually, the brief asks ME: \"What is the smallest edit that reverts the shift and still passes a detector row...?\" \u2014 so they want me to find the hole under B. Let me enumerate candidate escapes under B:\n\nReading 1 (log-grep, index+value): revert `>>1` to `>>3` fails (indices 1-4 with shifted values vs 4-7). Smallest escaping edit: `(relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3` then scale by 4: `(((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) << 2)` \u2014 wait, that's not a revert, it's semantically equal to >>1 on aligned offsets. As established, on 8-aligned offsets (all guest-reachable), (x>>3)<<2 == x>>1. So this edit passes the +0x08 oracle while being \"a revert of the shift token\". But semantically it's not a behavior revert on the reachable set \u2014 it IS a revert of the textual fix. And notably, it's ALSO bounds-safe at unaligned offsets (max index 108+3=111), unlike >>1 itself! So the \"escape\" is actually a semantically superior fix on the unaligned set too (no OOB even for put d). That's a delicious observation: the hole in B's oracle admits an edit that is strictly safer than the fix. Which means the oracle can't distinguish the fix from a different, arguably-better fix \u2014 but that's not a revert hazard, that's just weak specificity. The REAL revert (plain `>> 3`) fails the oracle. So under reading 1, B has NO hole that admits a genuine revert: plain revert fails; the only passing \"revert\" is behavior-preserving-on-reachable-offsets. Whereas A's hole (delete alignment conjunct) admits an edit that genuinely re-opens the OOB. So A's hole is strictly worse.\n\nHold on, wait. Let me double check the claim \"(x>>3)<<2 == x>>1 for 8-aligned x\". x = 8k. x>>3 = k. k<<2 = 4k. x>>1 = 4k. Yes. And for the store at +0x08: (8>>3)<<2 = 1<<2 = 4. 8>>1 = 4. Identical. And the SUBSEQUENT loop writes tlbi, tlbi+1, ... \u2014 same. So yes.\n\nBut also under reading 1, if the grep checks only index (not value), and the grep index is 4 (overlap), then plain >>3 passes \u2192 the oracle is vacuous under BOTH A and B. The brief says \"one seat already found that\" for A's hole \u2014 deleting the alignment conjunct. So the author is aware of A's hole and asks whether B has an analogous one.\n\nReading 2 (source-grep for `>> 1` textual): under B, smallest revert passing the grep: put `>> 1` in the shipped comment while code says `>> 3`. But B REQUIRES a comment! \"Ship the shift alone, with a comment.\" If the comment describes the fix (\"index is halfword offset: >> 1\"), a future revert of the code token that leaves the comment passes a textual grep for `>> 1`. Hmm \u2014 but would the detector grep for `>> 1`? \"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 the index. Plausibly `re_tex` and `>> 1`. Under this reading, B's comment creates the escape hatch: the comment containing `>> 1` satisfies the grep after the code reverts. Smallest edit: change the code token `>> 1` \u2192 `>> 3` (one token), leave the comment. Actually under this reading, the SMALLEST edit reverting the shift that still passes = flip the token; the comment does the rest. But wait \u2014 would that pass the behavioral part too? The behavioral part (store at +0x08) would then show indices 1..4 in the log... but under reading 2 the behavioral part is a store and the grep is over SOURCE. Hmm, but then what does the behavioral store verify? Maybe the behavioral store just verifies the arm doesn't decline/fatal (it's a write arm; the read arm fatals). Store at +0x08 succeeds under both >>3 and >>1. So the behavioral part doesn't discriminate; the source grep is the discriminator. Then under reading 2, the token-flip + comment passes BOTH parts. So B's hole under reading 2: the required comment can carry the grep-able text after a revert. Mitigation: don't put the literal grep target in the comment, or make the grep anchored to the code line (e.g., grep for `re_tex\\[tlbi\\]` with the `>> 1` on the same line... the index expression is on the tlbi line, not the store line \u2014 hmm, `int tlbi = ((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff;` \u2014 the fix changes 3 to 1 on the tlbi line; the store line has `d->re_tex[tlbi]`. A grep for \"array name and index\" \u2014 maybe it greps for `re_tex[tlbi]` and separately for `>> 1`?).\n\nI can't settle the detector's exact grep from the packet. I should say so and give the analysis under each reading, and note what fact would settle it (the detector row's exact grep pattern and whether it checks values/indices beyond the overlap).\n\nAlso \u2014 one more possible hole under B, reading 1: the detector stores at +0x08 only. Under B (no guard), could a revert be masked by choosing an edit that agrees with >>1 at +0x08 but not elsewhere? The (x>>3)<<2 edit agrees at ALL aligned offsets, so a+0x08-only oracle is fooled. But as noted, that edit is not a behavioral revert on the guest-reachable set. What about an edit that agrees at +0x08 ONLY and reverts elsewhere? E.g., `tlbi = (relative_addr & 0xff) >> 1;` \u2192 `tlbi = ((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3;` At +0x08: 1+3 = 4 \u2713. At +0x10: 2+3 = 5 vs 8 \u2717 (reverts!). At 0xd8: 27+3 = 30 vs 108 \u2014 reverts everywhere else. And bounds: max 27+3+3 = 33 \u2014 in bounds. So `>> 3 + 3` \u2014 wait, precedence: `>>` lower than `+`? In C, `+` binds tighter than `>>`. So `(x >> 3 + 3)` is `x >> 6`! Careful. Written as `((x >> 3) + 3)` it's the +3 edit. That edit passes a +0x08-only oracle and reverts all other offsets. Smallest edit: change `>> 1` to `>> 3) + 3` \u2014 hmm, token-wise: `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3 & 0xff`... anyway. So under B with a single-offset oracle, escapes exist: `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3` passes at +0x08 (index 4) and reverts elsewhere. But does the grep check the VALUE at index 4? If yes: under the fix, index 4 gets the FIRST halfword (data[0]*256+data[1]). Under `((x>>3)+3)`: tlbi starts at 4 \u2192 index 4 gets the first halfword too! Same value! So it passes even a value-checking oracle at +0x08. And it reverts every other aligned offset (e.g., +0x10 \u2192 5 instead of 8). So YES \u2014 under B, if the oracle anchors only +0x08, the edit `>> 1` \u2192 `>> 3) + 3` (i.e., `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3`) reverts the fix at every offset except +0x08 and passes the oracle including value checks. Hmm wait, but does it pass at +0x08 for the SUBSEQUENT indices? Oracle greps \"the array name and index\" \u2014 maybe it checks all four lines: indices 4,5,6,7 with the four halfwords. Under the +3 edit: indices 4,5,6,7 with the four halfwords \u2014 identical to the fix at +0x08! Because base index 4 either way. Yes \u2014 at +0x08, (8>>3)+3 = 1+3 = 4 = 8>>1. So the ENTIRE trace at +0x08 is identical. The oracle cannot distinguish. And at every other offset it's the old >>3 behavior shifted by 3 (still in bounds, max 30+3=33). So B's analogous hole: a single-anchored-offset oracle is fooled by an index expression that agrees with the fix at the anchor and reverts elsewhere. The fix's discriminating power at ONE offset is only one linear constraint on the index map; infinitely many expressions satisfy it.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 is that a meaningful \"hole\"? Under A, the found hole is: deleting the guard conjunct escapes (the guard is un-anchored). Under B, the analogous hole is: the oracle anchors only the shift AT ONE OFFSET; an edit that preserves the anchor but reverts elsewhere escapes. Both are real. But there's an asymmetry: A's hole requires deleting a protection that the oracle never covered \u2014 the escape RESTORES an OOB (well, restores OOB reachability via put d). B's hole requires a contrived index expression (`>>3)+3`) \u2014 the escape restores wrong-indexing (but not OOB! `((x>>3)+3)` max = 30, writes up to 33 \u2014 in bounds). So under B, the escaping edit is still bounds-safe; under A, the escaping edit re-opens the OOB. Actually wait \u2014 under A, if you delete the alignment conjunct, the shift is still >>1, so unaligned stores OOB. Yes.\n\nHmm, but actually under B, can an escape re-open OOB? The oracle at +0x08 can't see unaligned behavior at all (it doesn't test it). Under B, an editor could revert to plain >>3 \u2014 bounds-safe anyway (27..30). Under B, ANY escape's worst case is wrong indexing, not OOB, because... no wait: under B, an editor could change >>1 to something OOB-producing, e.g., `<< 3`. But that's not a \"revert\". The question is about edits that REVERT the shift. The shift's bug (>>3) is an under-index, bounds-safe. So reverting the shift under B cannot produce OOB \u2014 the pre-fix code was bounds-safe. The guard question (A) is about OOB. So under B, revert-risk = correctness-of-indexing only; under A, revert-risk includes both the shift (anchored at +0x08) and the guard (NOT anchored). Under A, the guard is the un-anchored addition; its deletion re-opens the exact OOB the round is about, invisibly to the oracle. That's the sharpest way to state it: A adds a component the natural oracle cannot witness; B adds none. An unwitnessed component is exactly what the prior LINEAR round refused to add.\n\nNow the deeper structural point connecting Q1/Q2/Q3:\n\nThe LINEAR precedent: prior round considered the same invariant, same put d exception, chose document-and-file over guard. The author moved A\u2192B on that precedent. But note: B and the LINEAR choice differ: LINEAR chose document-and-file for an EXISTING over-run; B ships a code change (the shift) plus comment, and the shift itself CREATES the filed-class member at TEX (via unaligned put d). Position C (ship nothing) is the true analogue of the LINEAR choice at TEX... no wait. At TEX today, with >>3, there is NO over-run (bounds-safe unconditionally). The >>1 fix introduces the over-run-under-unaligned. So the choices:\n\n- A: fix indexing + guard against the newly-introduced OOB. (code + code)\n- B: fix indexing + comment documenting the unaligned hazard; OOB newly introduced and left unguarded, filed by analogy? The brief says B = \"shift alone, with a comment\". The comment would presumably document that unaligned len-8 stores (put d only) overrun into re_linear_a, mirroring sgilinearoob.\n- C: no change; the mis-indexing stays (re_tex[1..4] instead of [4..7] etc.), no reader, so no observable severity.\n\nThe disanalogy question (Q2): at LINEAR the over-run exists today (inherited); at TEX it would be newly introduced by the fix. Is creating a member of a filed defect class categorically different from inheriting one?\n\nArguments that it IS different (i.e., against the author's inclination): the fork is a HARDENED fork; the round is a \"repair\" round; a repair that introduces a new latent OOB write (even unreachable via guest) is a regression by the fork's own standard \u2014 the defect class being filed doesn't neutralize the fact that the pre-repair code could not OOB and the post-repair code can. \"Filed\" for LINEAR is a judgment about pre-existing debt; \"filed\" for TEX would be a judgment about debt newly issued by the maintainer. There's a real distinction between accepting inherited risk and manufacturing it. Also the filing of sgilinearoob presumably comes with the guest-path argument; the TEX over-run would have the identical argument \u2014 so identical treatment is at least consistent. The consistency argument (B's core) is: same invariant, same sole exception, same class, same file \u2192 same disposition (document + file). The disanalogy argument (against B, for A or for C): the dispositions available are not just {guard, document}; at LINEAR, the over-run could not be removed without... hmm, actually could it? At LINEAR, the over-run exists today \u2014 under what indexing? We don't know the LINEAR arms' code from the packet. The packet doesn't inline the LINEAR arms, only says they carry the comment and the over-run stays filed. So I cannot check whether LINEAR's over-run was removable by a correct-indexing fix or inherent to the hardware semantics (e.g., the real CRIME linear TLB register map might genuinely overflow \u2014 the hardware might alias). NOT SETTLEABLE from the packet: whether LINEAR's over-run is a bug-fixable indexing error or an inherent map property. That matters: if LINEAR's over-run is inherent (hardware really does wrap/alias and the emulation faithfully overruns), then filing is forced and the analogy to TEX is weak, because TEX's over-run under >>1 is NOT forced \u2014 it's an artifact reachable only through a path (put d) that bypasses the machine's alignment contract. Whereas if LINEAR's over-run is also an artifact of a non-guest path, the analogy is strong. What single fact would settle it: the index computation and window size of the LINEAR arms (whether a correct-indexing edit exists for LINEAR that is bounds-safe under put d).\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Actually, at LINEAR the over-run \"exists today\" \u2014 the packet says \"the file's own sibling arms have an over-run of this exact class today\". \"This exact class\" = unaligned 8-byte store overrunning an in-struct array via put d. So at LINEAR, presumably, the indexing is CORRECT (>>1-like?) and the over-run arises from unaligned put d stores. That would make the analogy very strong: correct indexing + unguarded + put d-exception documented + filed. If LINEAR's indexing is correct and the over-run is exactly the put d unaligned case, then position A at TEX (guard) would make TEX stricter than LINEAR for no stated reason \u2014 the file would carry two dispositions for the same class. But the packet does NOT inline the LINEAR code, so I can't verify that LINEAR's over-run is via unaligned-only. The packet says \"an over-run of this exact class today\" \u2014 I'll take that as READ (the author asserts the class match). But what I can't verify: whether LINEAR's over-run is ALSO reachable only via put d, or also via some guest path. The packet says the guest-path invariant is \"stated for GUEST paths only\" in the comment and put d is the one exception \u2014 implying at LINEAR too, put d is the only trigger. So the analogy holds as far as the packet states.\n\nThen the disanalogy (create vs inherit): the author asks to be hard on it. Let me think about what's really at stake.\n\nThe invariant: guest paths cannot deliver unaligned len==8. If TRUE and STABLE, then >>1 is bounds-safe on all guest-reachable executions, and the OOB is reachable only through the debugger's deliberately-unaligned write. The debugger is a human operator tool; a human doing `put d` at an unaligned CRIME_RE_TEX address gets silent corruption of re_linear_a. Severity: re_linear_a presumably IS read (it's the linear TLB, and there are two LINEAR arms that use it \u2014 actually the LINEAR arms presumably READ re_linear_a for translation? If re_linear_a is used for DMA translation, corrupting it = memory corruption visible to guest). Hmm wait \u2014 that's important! The TEX over-run lands in re_linear_a. If re_linear_a has readers (the LINEAR arms implement translation using it), then the put d OOB corrupts a LIVE structure. But via put d only. And the LINEAR filed defect (sgilinearoob) presumably overruns re_linear_a from the other side or some other array... The packet says the struct declares re_tex[112] immediately followed by re_linear_a[32]. So OOB writes at 112..114 land in the first 8 bytes of re_linear_a[0..1]. Is re_linear_a read? Not stated! The packet says re_tex has NO reader. It says nothing about re_linear_a readers. If re_linear_a is read by the LINEAR arms (plausible \u2014 \"re_linear_a\" = linear translation A table), then the over-run corrupts a structure with live readers. NOT SETTLEABLE from the packet: whether re_linear_a has readers. If it does, the \"silent, in-struct, invisible\" OOB has a real (if debugger-only) consequence channel; if it doesn't, it's equally dead. What fact would settle: presence of reads of re_linear_a. This matters for severity of the newly-introduced OOB, and hence for Q2's force. Though \u2014 under B the OOB is only via put d, which the prior round already declared laundering-not-witnessing. The prior round's stance: put d is out of scope for repros. Does that also mean put d is out of scope for HAZARD? That's a subtle asymmetry worth flagging: the prior round used the put d exclusion to reject a REPRO (evidence), not to dismiss a HAZARD. Using \"put d can't witness\" to also mean \"put d can't harm\" is an extrapolation \u2014 the comment excluded put d as evidence, not as a threat model. A debugger that silently corrupts emulator state on an unaligned poke is a real (if low) hazard \u2014 GXemul's debugger is a human interface; humans make unaligned pokes and the warning is a printf. But: the hazard pre-exists at LINEAR (filed). So the question is only about ADDING a new instance. Adding a new instance of a filed, accepted, human-only hazard, in exchange for fixing a guest-visible mis-indexing... wait, is the >>3 mis-indexing guest-visible? re_tex has NO READER (point 5). So TODAY, nothing is observable: no reader of re_tex, and >>3 is bounds-safe. The fix's benefit is CORRECTNESS-FOR-THE-FUTURE (when sgirekills creates the first reader). The fix's cost is a new put d-only OOB instance.\n\nSo actually the whole round is: make re_tex correctly indexed in advance of its first reader (prerequisite of sgirekills), at the price of introducing a put d-only over-run instance (unless guarded, position A).\n\nQ3: C says ship nothing, but the row is a PREREQUISITE of sgirekills (HIGH). If re_tex keeps >>3 and sgirekills adds a reader, the reader reads mis-indexed entries \u2014 wait, no: the reader would read whatever was written; the mis-indexing means stores at offset X land at index (X>>3)..(+3) instead of (X>>1)..(+3). A reader that computes the same wrong index would be self-consistent; a reader computing the RIGHT index would disagree with the store. sgirekills would presumably add a reader with correct indexing \u2192 mismatch with the >>3 store \u2192 guest-visible wrong translation... but only when the reader exists. So C (ship nothing) is coherent ONLY if sgirekills also doesn't ship, or if sgirekills' reader uses the same >>3 convention (laundering the bug into a convention). The tension: \"do not ship\" vs \"runs first\". Resolution options:\n\n1. C is wrong because the row is a prerequisite: the >>3 mis-indexing is not observable today but becomes load-bearing once the reader exists; fixing indexing BEFORE the first reader exists is the cheapest moment (no reader = no behavioral delta to audit); shipping the reader first would bake the mismatch in.\n2. C is right and the prerequisite ruling should be revisited/amended: the prerequisite is not \"ship a fix\" but \"resolve the row\" \u2014 a row can be resolved by a DECISION (document + file + defer to sgirekills round, where the fix and the reader ship together and the oracle can witness both). That's actually a clean resolution: merge this fix INTO sgirekills, so the store fix and the reader ship atomically and the detector witnesses the pair. Then \"do not ship this round\" and \"prerequisite satisfied\" are reconciled by deferral-with-coupling, not by shipping a no-reader fix now.\n\nHmm, but the brief says a prior ruling made this row a PREREQUISITE of sgirekills. If this round ships nothing, sgirekills is blocked (can't run first...). \"Do not ship\" and \"runs first\" are in tension. Resolve explicitly: either (a) ship B now (satisfies prerequisite; the smallest correct-indexing change; the OOB instance is put d-only, same disposition as LINEAR), or (b) amend the prerequisite: fold the store fix into the sgirekills row so store+reader+oracle ship together; the prerequisite was presumably \"re_tex indexing must be correct before a reader exists\" \u2014 folding satisfies the spirit and eliminates the no-witness window. Option (b) is attractive: it eliminates the \"fix with empty guest delta and no witness\" problem (B's acknowledged weakness: no reader \u2192 no behavioral oracle possible beyond the debug print; the detector row uses put d or...? wait, the detector row \"stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08\" \u2014 via WHAT? If via put d, the prior round's comment says put d can't witness! If the detector row stores via put d, it CONTRADICTS the LINEAR comment's methodology. If via guest code, the guest would need an aligned sd to CRIME_RE_TEX+base+0x08 \u2014 possible: write a guest snippet doing aligned stores. The packet doesn't say how the detector row delivers the store! That matters: if the detector uses put d, it re-imports the witnessing question the file's own comment excluded. Flag it.)\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 \"detector row that stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08 and greps the array name and index\" \u2014 in a regression-detector suite (this smells like a \"detector rows\" framework \u2014 behavioral tests with greps). The store could be delivered by a guest MIPS program (aligned sd) \u2014 that's the clean way and consistent with the put d exclusion. Not settleable from the packet; name it.\n\nNow Q1: Which position ships? Let me actually reason to a verdict.\n\nPosition A's core: \"make the precondition local rather than inherited from an invariant argued in another file and known to have an exception.\" The put d exception is real (READ, point 4). The invariant (point 3) is argued for guest paths and has exactly one known exception. A guard makes TEX robust regardless of path. Cost: one conjunct; the addition licence question (position B claims the conjunct is an ADDITION and additions are governed by an addition licence \u2014 i.e., the round's mandate covers CORRECTIONS, and additions need separate licence). The brief references \"the addition licence governs ADDITIONS; the conjunct IS an addition, and its guest delta is EMPTY\". I can't verify the licence terms from the packet \u2014 the licence text is not inlined. So whether the conjunct is licensable is NOT SETTLEABLE from the packet. What would settle it: the licence's wording re: guards/conjuncts on corrected lines vs new statements. A one-token change to an existing condition vs a new `if` \u2014 the conjunct `(relative_addr & 7)` modifies an existing condition (`len != 8`), it doesn't add a statement. Whether modifying an existing condition counts as \"addition\" depends on licence wording \u2014 packet doesn't include it. Flag.\n\nPosition B's core: shift alone + comment, consistent with LINEAR precedent (document-and-file for put d-only over-runs). B's weakness: it INTRODUCES an OOB (put d-only) where none existed. The prior precedent filed an EXISTING over-run; B manufactures a new one. Q2 asks if that's categorically different. I think there IS a real asymmetry, and the honest answer is: yes, creating a member of a filed class is different from inheriting one \u2014 \"filed\" status for LINEAR is a record of debt; filing a debt you just created in a hardening round is odd \u2014 BUT the alternative dispositions both have worse problems:\n\n- Guarding (A) contradicts the file's own recorded judgment for the identical hazard class in the same file, creating a consistency problem (two arms, same hazard, opposite dispositions) \u2014 UNLESS A's guard is justified by the create-vs-inherit distinction itself: i.e., \"we guard the NEW instance because we're creating it, and we don't retrofit the OLD instance because it predates us.\" That's actually a coherent position! The distinction licenses differential treatment: new debt gets a guard at creation (cheap, one line, same PR), old debt stays filed (retrofitting is a separate round). Hmm \u2014 but then A must ALSO say the LINEAR decision was not wrong, merely not retrofitted. The brief asks: \"If A, say whether the prior LINEAR decision is wrong or merely distinguishable.\" So A can answer: LINEAR is distinguishable-by-origin: inherited \u2192 filed; created \u2192 guarded-at-birth. Is that principled or convenient? It's the exact question Q2 poses. Let me be hard on it.\n\nThe steelman for \"create \u2260 inherit\": In a hardening fork, the baseline is \"no NEW OOB writes reachable by any path\". A repair that introduces a new OOB write is a net regression on the fork's own metric unless paired with a guard. The LINEAR filing is grand-fathered debt with a recorded decision; the fork's standard for NEW code (and repairs are new code) can be stricter without indicting the old decision. Precedent governs like cases; create-vs-inherit is a relevant dissimilarity, so the precedent doesn't force B. Also: the LINEAR comment's put d exclusion was about WITNESSING (repro methodology), and using it to justify CREATING a new hazard is a scope extension of that ruling \u2014 the comment says \"don't use put d to build a repro\", not \"put d hazards don't count\". B leans on the second, stronger claim, which the comment doesn't actually make. THAT is a real argumentative defect in B: it converts an evidentiary exclusion (\"put d can't witness\") into a safety exclusion (\"put d can't harm\"). The packet's quote supports only the former. READ-check: the quoted comment text: \"put d must NOT be used to 'witness' the over-run: it bypasses the guest instruction path, so a reproduction built on it would be laundering rather than witnessing.\" Yes \u2014 that's purely about reproduction/witnessing. B's use of the precedent to justify INTRODUCING an over-run goes beyond the comment's stated scope. However \u2014 the precedent ALSO includes \"the over-run stays FILED, not guarded\", which IS a disposition about the hazard, not just the repro. So the precedent does contain a hazard-disposition (file, don't guard) for a put d-only OOB. So B's analogy isn't purely the witnessing quote; it's the filed-not-guarded disposition. The create/inherit asymmetry then does the work: does filed-not-guarded extend to newly-created instances? That's the crux, and the packet cannot settle it; it's a judgment call. My job: lay out what each answer commits you to.\n\nLet me now think about what verdict I actually want to give.\n\nGiven:\n- The mis-indexing (>>3) is real and agreed (all seats).\n- re_tex has no reader today; severity nil today.\n- The row is a prerequisite of a HIGH row that creates the first reader.\n- The fix (>>1) is correct on the guest-reachable set (aligned) and introduces a put d-only OOB at unaligned offsets (max 3 halfwords into re_linear_a).\n- Prior precedent in-file: identical class, filed-not-guarded, put d excluded as witness.\n- Licence for the conjunct: unknown from packet.\n\nOptions ranked:\n\nShip B (shift + comment + FILE the new OOB as a TEX-side instance, e.g., extend sgilinearoob or file sgitexoob), AND resolve Q3 by noting the prerequisite is satisfied in the sense that indexing is now correct for the future reader; the OOB instance joins the filed class with the same disposition as its sibling.\n\nOR ship A: shift + guard, distinguishing LINEAR as inherited-vs-created. A's cost: contradicts in-file precedent unless the distinction is articulated; licence unsettled; the guard is unwitnessable by the natural oracle (Q4's found hole) \u2014 an un-anchored guard is exactly the kind of addition that future rounds delete (one seat already demonstrated the deletion escapes the detector). That last point is strong: A adds a protection that the anchored oracle cannot see, in a codebase whose process evidently relies on detector rows to prevent reverts. An un-witnessed guard WILL be deletable without detection \u2014 the demonstrated escape proves the process can't defend it. So A's guard is not durable under the project's own revert-detection regime. That's a process argument against A that doesn't depend on the licence.\n\nWait, but hold on \u2014 is that right? The detector row greps \"the array name and index\". Could the detector row be EXTENDED to witness the guard? To witness the alignment conjunct, the detector would need an UNALIGNED len-8 store that gets declined \u2014 but delivering unaligned len-8 requires put d (the only known path), and the file's own comment forbids put d-based witnessing... but forbade it for the OVER-RUN repro. For a GUARD witness, put d would show the decline (no corruption). Hmm \u2014 actually using put d to witness a GUARD is different from using it to witness an over-run: the guard's effect is \"the store is declined\", observable as the value NOT appearing in re_tex. But wait \u2014 under the put d exclusion, is the decline even correct behavior? If put d pokes unaligned, should the device decline? A guard `if (len != 8 || (relative_addr & 7))` declines unaligned stores \u2014 for put d, that changes debugger behavior (the poke silently doesn't land... or is latched like the len != 8 decline). That's a real behavioral delta on the put d path \u2014 which B says has \"empty guest delta\" but NOT empty put d delta! Position B's claim \"its guest delta is EMPTY\" \u2014 the conjunct's guest delta is empty (guest can't produce unaligned len 8), but its PUT D delta is non-empty: unaligned pokes get declined instead of over-running. Whether that's desirable is the whole question. And it means the guard is witnessable ONLY via put d \u2014 the exact path the precedent excluded from witnessing. So A's guard can only be anchored by a detector row that the file's own methodology comment calls laundering. That's a genuine bind for A: its extra protection is both (a) un-witnessable by guest-legal detector rows, and (b) witnessable only by a method the project has ruled out. A is thus structurally un-anchorable in this process. That's the sharpest argument for B over A, and it's available entirely from the packet.\n\nHmm, although \u2014 one could argue the detector could check the SOURCE for the guard (grep for `& 7`). Source greps anchor deletions textually. But the found hole (deleting the alignment half) was said to escape the detector \u2014 so the detector row as specified doesn't grep for the guard. Could a future round add such a grep? Sure, but then the guard is anchored only textually, which is weak (comments/strings can satisfy greps) \u2014 same class of hole as Q4 discusses. And more fundamentally: a textually-anchored, behaviorally-unwitnessable guard protects nothing measurable; it's a talisman. The project's own prior round preferred documentation over talismans, apparently.\n\nNow B's weaknesses to be honest about:\n\n1. B introduces a new OOB instance (put d-only). If one holds \"no new OOB in a hardening fork, period\", B fails. The mitigation is filing it with the same disposition as LINEAR \u2014 consistent, but the create/inherit discomfort stands. I should state the discomfort plainly: the fix converts a bounds-safe-but-wrong index into a right index with a narrower unsafe fringe. The fringe is empty on guest paths (given premise 3) and non-empty only on the excluded path. If premise 3 ever breaks (a new CPU mode, a DMA engine that issues unaligned 8-byte writes to this window \u2014 WAIT. Premise 3 says \"MIPS guest paths\". What about DMA / device-initiated writes? CRIME is the memory controller on SGI machines \u2014 does anything else in the emulator write to CRIME_RE_TEX with len 8 unaligned? The premise only covers MIPS guest instruction paths and the debugger. What about other callers of memory_rw with this address range \u2014 e.g., an IO device doing DMA through CRIME? In GXemul, memory_rw is the central access path; devices call memory_rw... if some device or disk-DMA path issues unaligned 8-byte writes, premise 3's invariant doesn't cover it. The packet's premise 3 says \"MIPS guest paths cannot deliver\" \u2014 the strength of A's \"make it local\" is precisely that it doesn't depend on enumerating ALL paths. B inherits an invariant whose coverage is \"MIPS guest paths + debugger\" \u2014 what about the console, SCSI DMA, ethernet DMA writing through this window? CRIME_RE_TEX is a translation table for... \"re\" = rendering engine? Actually in GXemul, dev_sgi_re.c \u2014 hmm, \"SGI RE\"? Might be \"CRM/RE\" \u2014 CRIME is the system controller on SGI O2. \"RE\" might be... Actually I recall GXemul has dev_sgi_re.c implementing... I shouldn't speculate about hardware meaning. The point stands: premise 3 covers guest instruction paths; DMA-style callers (devices issuing memory_rw on guest memory containing the CRIME register block) are a third category not addressed by premises 3\u20134. If any in-emulator device can write into this MMIO window with unaligned len-8, then >>1 is exploitable-without-put-d and B's safety collapses; the guard (A) would cover it. NOT SETTLEABLE from the packet. The single fact that would settle it: whether any non-CPU caller of memory_rw can target the CRIME register window with an unaligned 8-byte access (e.g., DMA or other bus masters), or a list of all writers to this address range. Hmm \u2014 is DMA-to-MMIO-register-window even a thing here? Devices DMA to RAM typically, not to other devices' register windows. But \"typically\" is not an argument; the packet gives me no enumeration of callers. A's rationale (\"local rather than inherited\") is precisely robustness to unknown callers. This is the strongest remaining argument FOR A. I must present it fairly: A's locality is not paranoia if the caller set is unenumerated. And note the packet says the invariant is \"argued in another file\" \u2014 the argument lives elsewhere; a local guard doesn't depend on it. So the real A/B question reduces to: do you trust the invariant as a global property of all non-debugger writers? The packet cannot settle the completeness of the caller enumeration. Name it.\n\n2. B's licence claim: \"the addition licence governs ADDITIONS; the conjunct IS an addition\". Un-settleable (licence not inlined). Also contestable: is a conjunct on an existing condition an \"addition\"? Purely a definitional question about the licence \u2014 packet doesn't include it. If the licence is per-diff-line or per-token, the answer differs. Name it.\n\n3. Q3: B must reconcile with the prerequisite ruling: B ships, so the prerequisite is satisfied. Fine. C is the one in tension. If I ship B, Q3 resolves trivially: the row ships as B, prerequisite met, sgirekills unblocked. But I should address C's argument (\"re_tex has no reader \u2192 severity nil \u2192 any diff is strictly larger than nothing\"): C is wrong BECAUSE the row's value is not present-day severity but pre-positioning for sgirekills; the prerequisite ruling already decided that sequencing (the fix must exist before the reader). C relitigates that without addressing it. However, C contains a live insight: a fix with no reader has no behavioral witness other than the debug print; the detector row must store and grep the debug output. That insight shapes the oracle (Q4). Also C's \"any diff is strictly larger than doing nothing\" is only true if present-day risk is the only metric; the prerequisite ruling embodies a different metric (sequencing/coupling). So C should be recorded as rejected-on-the-prerequisite, and the rejection should note the tension the brief flags: the prior ruling stands unless explicitly revoked; if the panel wants C, it must ALSO amend the sgirekills prerequisite, otherwise a future round trips. Since I'm shipping B, no amendment needed. But wait \u2014 should I consider C seriously? Let me steelman C once more: severity nil today; the fix creates a (put d-only) OOB; doing nothing defers to the sgirekills round where fix+reader ship together with a real oracle (the reader witnesses the indexing!). Under C-then-merge, the indexing fix ships WITH its witness. That's arguably the cleanest engineering: no unwitnessed code ever exists. The counter: the prerequisite ruling exists precisely to prevent that? Why would a prior round make this row a prerequisite of sgirekills? Plausibly BECAUSE they wanted the store fixed before the reader arrives (so the reader's round doesn't have to touch two things, or so the mismatch can't be baked). If the ruling's rationale was \"the reader must not be built on a wrong store\", then merging the fix into sgirekills VIOLATES the spirit. But I don't know the ruling's rationale \u2014 not inlined. What would settle it: the prior ruling's text. Hmm \u2014 actually the brief says \"a prior ruling made this row a PREREQUISITE of a HIGH row (sgirekills, which would create re_tex's first reader)\". The rationale isn't given. So C-with-merge is viable IF the ruling permits folding; not settleable. But here's a pragmatic point: B is one token + comment; the cost of shipping B now is near-zero and it satisfies the ruling as-is; deferring requires amending a ruling (process cost, and the brief warns a future round would trip over un-resolved tension). Ship B.\n\nWait, but I should double-check B's \"guest delta is EMPTY\" claim \u2014 the brief attributes to B: \"the conjunct IS an addition, and its guest delta is EMPTY\". The conjunct's guest delta is empty given premise 3. But is premise 3 airtight? Premise 3: \"MIPS guest paths cannot deliver an unaligned len == 8. Ordinary ld/sd raise ADES/ADEL without calling memory_rw; the unaligned ldl/ldr/sdl/sdr helper masks the address itself.\" READ (as stated). Caveats I can raise from knowledge of MIPS/GXemul: (a) `ldl/ldr/sdl/sdr` \"masks the address\" \u2014 masks to alignment, so effective address is aligned \u2014 OK, so len-8 via ldl lands aligned. (b) What about `sd` on an address where the LOW bits are aligned but memory_rw is called with a non-8-aligned base because of... no, sd requires alignment or traps. (c) What about the kernel's uncached/KSEG access \u2014 same instruction path. (d) Multi-access paths: an 8-byte store delivered as two 4-byte or eight 1-byte accesses by some bus layer? Then len would be 4 or 1, not 8 \u2014 the len != 8 decline covers those. Interesting: the decline for len != 8 already handles split accesses; only exactly-8 unaligned is dangerous, and that's premise 3's case. (e) Big-endian vs little-endian guest: irrelevant to alignment. (f) FPU/store-conditional? sdxc1? movn... `sdc1` \u2014 double store \u2014 also alignment-checked. So premise 3 is solid for instruction-issued stores, AS STATED. The residual is non-instruction writers (DMA/other devices). Keep that flag.\n\nAlso \u2014 hmm, one more premise check. Premise 2: \">> 3 is bounds-safe UNCONDITIONALLY \u2014 indices 27..30 at every in-window offset.\" Verify: max offset 0xdf \u2192 0xdf>>3 = 27; +3 \u2192 30. re_tex[112] holds 0..111. 30 < 112 \u2713. But WAIT: is the window check `relative_addr < CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` on the START address only? An 8-byte store at offset 0xd8..0xdf is the last fully-inside; a store at 0xda starts in-window and its BYTES extend past 0xe0 \u2014 but the INDEX is computed from the start (27) and writes 27..30 \u2014 in bounds. The window overrun (bytes past 0xe0) doesn't touch the array indexing \u2014 data[] is 8 bytes supplied by the caller; the loop consumes exactly 8. So no issue for the array. But it does mean the \"window\" permits a store at 0xda..0xe1 \u2014 bytes at 0xe0-0xe1 belong to the NEXT register \u2014 but since only the START is checked and the whole 8 bytes funnel into re_tex, no cross-register corruption. Fine. Premise 2 stands (READ + arithmetic consistent).\n\nPremise 1: \"the halfword index of a byte offset is offset >> 1. Three sibling uint16_t arms use >> 1; the window is 224 bytes = 112 halfwords = re_tex[112].\" Arithmetic: 0xe0 = 224 \u2713; 224/2 = 112 \u2713. Consistent. But note the code has `& 0xff` BEFORE the shift: `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3)`. If CRIME_RE_TEX is not 0-mod-256, the `& 0xff` does NOT subtract the window base! E.g., if CRIME_RE_TEX = 0x100 (mod 256 = 0), fine. If CRIME_RE_TEX \u2261 0x40 (mod 256), then relative_addr & 0xff \u2260 offset-in-window, and BOTH >>3 and >>1 mis-index. The packet never states CRIME_RE_TEX's value! The fix >>1 is only correct if `(relative_addr & 0xff)` equals the in-window offset, i.e., CRIME_RE_TEX \u2261 0 (mod 256) \u2014 actually mod 256 exactly, since the mask is 0xff. If CRIME_RE_TEX is, say, 0x1000, then relative_addr & 0xff = in-window offset \u2713. If it's 0x1040, then at relative_addr = 0x1040 (offset 0), & 0xff gives 0x40 \u2192 index 0x20 under >>1 \u2014 WRONG by 0x20. So the correctness of the ENTIRE fix (and of the claim \"indices 27..30\" / \"111..114\") presupposes CRIME_RE_TEX \u2261 0 mod 256. The packet states the window as [CRIME_RE_TEX, CRIME_RE_TEX+0xe0) and asserts the index behaviors \u2014 the assertions are consistent ONLY under the mod-256 alignment. NOT SETTLEABLE from the packet: the value of CRIME_RE_TEX. Single fact that settles it: the numeric value of the CRIME_RE_TEX macro. If it's not 256-aligned, the fix needs `(relative_addr - CRIME_RE_TEX) >> 1` instead \u2014 and the bounds analysis changes too (mask 0xff with a non-aligned base could even produce OOB under >>1 at MORE offsets... let me check: if base \u2261 0x40 mod 256, then in-window relative_addr & 0xff ranges over [0x40, 0x11f mod 256] = [0x40,0xff]\u222a[0x00,0x1f] \u2014 max 0xff>>1 = 127 \u2192 writes up to 130 \u2014 OOB even ALIGNED! So if CRIME_RE_TEX is not 256-aligned, >>1 with the & 0xff mask is OOB even on guest-aligned stores, and premise 2's \"bounds-safe only under 8-alignment\" is wrong \u2014 it'd be unsafe at aligned offsets too. THE WHOLE ANALYSIS HINGES ON THE MACRO'S VALUE. This is a big catch \u2014 a premise the packet treats as established (\"AGREED FACTS\") actually contains an unstated dependency. The brief says \"Check the premises, not the arithmetic\" \u2014 ha, but this is a premise-check: the agreed facts' index computations silently assume CRIME_RE_TEX mod 256 == 0. All seats agreed on the indices; if they all read the tree, presumably the macro IS 256-aligned and they verified it. But from the packet alone, it's an assumption. I should flag it as the one premise that everything hangs on and that the packet does not state. Also the trailing `& 0xff` after the shift: `>> 3) & 0xff` \u2014 masks the index to 8 bits; with >>1 the max index is 111 < 255 so the mask is inert... unless CRIME_RE_TEX isn't 256-aligned as above. Also \u2014 should the fix also drop the `& 0xff` masks? Not necessary if base is 256-aligned. Under B (one-token change), leaving `& 0xff` is harmless given alignment. OK.\n\nAnother premise check: premise 2 says at offset 0xdf, >>1 writes indices 111,112,113,114. 0xdf>>1 = 111 \u2713 (223/2 = 111). +3 \u2192 114 \u2713. And \"the struct declares uint16_t re_tex[112]; immediately followed by uint32_t re_linear_a[32];\" \u2014 READ. OOB halfwords 112..114 = bytes 224..229 relative to re_tex start \u2014 overlapping re_linear_a[0] (bytes 0..3) and re_linear_a[1] (bytes 4..7) partially: halfword 112 = bytes 224-225 = first half of re_linear_a[0]; 113 = second half of [0]; 114 = first half of [1]. Fine, consistent with \"lands in the linear TLB\". \u2713.\n\nPremise 4: put d prints a warning (printf, not a guard) and proceeds to store_64bit_word. READ. Note: store_64bit_word presumably delivers len=8 in one call. Consistent.\n\nPremise 5: three occurrences of re_tex in the whole tree: declaration, this store, a debug() on the next line. READ. \"Observable severity today is nil\" \u2014 REASONED from 5: with no reader, no guest-visible effect; agreed, though the debug print is an observable (a human reading logs). OK.\n\nNow the LINEAR precedent (the in-tree fact): I take as READ (author verified). But note what the packet does NOT give: the LINEAR arms' code, sgilinearoob's filing text, and whether LINEAR's over-run is put d-only. The author asserts \"an over-run of this exact class today\". I'll trust as READ-ish (author-verified) but note the analogy's load-bearing element: that LINEAR's over-run is ALSO unaligned-put-d-only. If LINEAR's over-run is reachable by GUEST paths (e.g., its window/index math overruns even aligned), then \"this exact class\" is false and the precedent is much weaker (a guest-reachable OOB filed-not-guarded would be a different, scarier precedent). The packet's point 3 framing (\"stated for GUEST paths only\" + put d the one exception) implies put d-only, but for the TEX arms; for LINEAR, the comment \"considered exactly this question\" \u2014 I'll take the author's word but flag the unverifiable element: whether sgilinearoob's trigger set is identical (put d-only). Single fact: the LINEAR arms' index computation + window size, or the sgilinearoob filing's trigger analysis.\n\nNow let me also sanity-check position A's guard semantics: `if (len != 8 || (relative_addr & 7))` decline \u2014 wait, the existing code already declines len != 8. The guard ADDS `(relative_addr & 7)`. But careful: relative_addr & 7 tests alignment relative to the DEVICE base, which equals in-window offset alignment iff CRIME_RE_TEX \u2261 0 mod 8. Same macro dependency. If CRIME_RE_TEX is 8-aligned (surely) but not 256-aligned, then `(relative_addr & 7)` is still correct for alignment-in-window iff base \u2261 0 mod 8. The >>1 fix needs base \u2261 0 mod 256 (because of the 0xff mask) OR dropping the mask in favor of subtracting the base. Worth one line.\n\nAlso: under A, the decline path \u2014 \"decline, latched\" \u2014 the existing len!=8 decline latches something. The unaligned decline would presumably share it. Behavior delta on put d: an unaligned poke gets latched-declined instead of partially overrunning. Fine.\n\nNow, Q4 detailed answer. Let me settle on the most defensible reading. The brief: \"a detector row that stores 8 bytes at in-window +0x08 and greps the array name and index\". I'll treat it as: the row performs an 8-byte store at in-window offset 0x08 (delivery path unspecified in the packet) and greps (log output, presumably the debug() line) for the array name and the index. The natural oracle: fix writes halfwords to indices 4..7 at +0x08; buggy code writes 1..4. The anchor index is presumably 4 (first halfword's landing) \u2014 but index 4 is in BOTH traces (overlap!). So:\n\n- If the grep is `re_tex[4]` + value-of-first-halfword: discriminates \u2713 (buggy puts 4th halfword at 4).\n- If the grep is `re_tex[4]` without value: VACUOUS \u2014 passes the full revert `>>1 \u2192 >>3` under BOTH A and B. The overlap at the anchor makes the oracle blind. This is a hole SHARED by A and B, and it's in the ORACLE, not the position. The one-seat-found A-hole (delete alignment conjunct) is additional to this.\n- If the grep anchors index 5/6/7 or the full set {4,5,6,7}: discriminates \u2713 (buggy writes {1,2,3,4}).\n\nThe packet says \"greps the array name and index\" (singular index) \u2014 so plausibly ONE index. If that index is 4, the oracle is vacuous as specified. I should flag: the anchor must be an index OUTSIDE the overlap ({5,6,7}) or include the value. Otherwise even the plain revert passes. This is a defect in the proposed oracle independent of A/B \u2014 worth stating as my Q4 headline, THEN answer B's analogous hole.\n\nUnder B, analogous hole (given a DISCRIMINATING oracle anchored at +0x08 only): the smallest edit that reverts the shift's behavior at all OTHER offsets while passing at +0x08: change the index expression to agree at 0x08 and disagree elsewhere. Smallest token-wise: `>> 1` \u2192 `>> 3) + 3` is not smaller; hmm what about just `>> 3` + something? Let's find the minimal-diff candidates:\n\n(i) `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 1) & 0xff` \u2192 `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3` \u2014 tokens changed: `1` \u2192 `3) + 3` \u2014 adds tokens. Not minimal.\n\n(ii) Revert to `>> 3` and change the LOOP to compensate at the anchor? The loop is shared.\n\n(iii) `>> 1` \u2192 `>> 3` with `tlbi++` \u2192 `tlbi += 4`? At +0x08: base 1, writes 1, 5, 9, 13 \u2014 the SET is {1,5,9,13} vs fix {4,5,6,7}: index 5 present in both! If oracle greps index 5 alone, this passes! Ha. But if it greps value@5: fix puts 2nd halfword at 5; edit puts 2nd halfword at 5 too (1\u2192first, 5\u2192second, 9\u2192third, 13\u2192fourth)! Wait: edit: tlbi starts 1, writes re_tex[1]=hw0, then tlbi += 4 \u2192 re_tex[5]=hw1, re_tex[9]=hw2, re_tex[13]=hw3. Fix: re_tex[4]=hw0, [5]=hw1, [6]=hw2, [7]=hw3. If oracle greps name+index 5 (+value hw1): BOTH match! If oracle greps index 4+value: edit fails (fix has 4=hw0; edit has no write at 4 \u2014 the ORIGINAL buggy value remains... wait, under the edit, was index 4 written? No. The grep for `re_tex[4]` in the LOG after the store \u2014 the debug prints only what was stored this time? \"a debug() on the next line\" \u2014 inside the loop presumably, printing each stored halfword. So the log shows only stored entries. Edit log: 1,5,9,13. Fix log: 4,5,6,7.) So the discriminating power depends entirely on which index is grepped. The brief says THE index (one). Whatever single index k is grepped, an escaping edit needs to write hw_i at index k for the i the oracle expects. Since the oracle stores 8 bytes = 4 halfwords, and the escape controls both base and stride (via tlbi++ changes) \u2014 the escape space is large. With base b and stride s, writes (b, b+s, b+2s, b+3s). Fix: (4,5,6,7). For any single anchored (index, value) pair (k, hw_i), the escape `>> 3` with stride 4: (1,5,9,13) covers (5, hw1) \u2014 i.e., if the oracle anchors the SECOND halfword's landing (index 5), the `>>3 + tlbi+=4` escape passes. If it anchors the FIRST (4, hw0), that escape fails, but `((x>>3)+3)` = (4,5,6,7) \u2014 identical to fix at the anchor! And that one reverts ALL other offsets (at 0x10: 5..8 vs fix 8..11 \u2014 wait (0x10>>3)+3 = 2+3 = 5, writes 5,6,7,8; fix writes 8,9,10,11 \u2014 overlap at 8! Hmm interesting, partial overlaps everywhere). Whatever \u2014 the point stands: a single-offset, single-index oracle can be satisfied by a family of expressions that revert the shift's semantics elsewhere. The smallest such edit for the likely anchor (4, hw0): `>> 1` \u2192 `>> 3) + 3` OR `>> 3` with loop stride 4 IF anchored at (5,hw1). Both are two-ish-token edits. So YES, B has an analogous hole \u2014 BUT with two mitigating differences: (a) the escaping edits are not the plain revert; a lazy future editor flipping `1`\u2192`3` gets CAUGHT (the plain revert fails the oracle), whereas under A the demonstrated escape IS the lazy edit (delete a conjunct \u2014 a natural \"cleanup\" that a future round might do believing the invariant). The B escapes require deliberately crafting an index expression that agrees at the anchor \u2014 that's adversarial, not accidental. Revert-risk is dominated by accidental/ordinary edits; B's oracle catches the ordinary revert; A's oracle misses the ordinary deletion (conjunct-deletion is exactly the kind of edit \"simplification\" rounds do). (b) Under B, the escaping edits are all still BOUNDS-SAFE at every offset? Check `>>3)+3`: max (0xdf>>3)+3 = 30, writes \u2264 33 \u2713 in bounds. `>>3` stride 4: 1,5,9,13... base max 27 \u2192 27,31,35,39 \u2713. So B's holes cannot reintroduce OOB; A's hole (deleting the conjunct, keeping >>1) reintroduces the OOB exactly. Actually hmm, wait: under A, the escape deletes the alignment conjunct \u2014 with >>1 still in place, unaligned put d \u2192 OOB. Yes. So: A's hole reopens the safety defect; B's hole only mis-indexes. In a hardening fork, the safety-relevant hole is worse. So B's oracle-hole is strictly less dangerous.\n\nBUT the shared vacuity risk (overlap at index 4) applies to both: if the oracle greps name+index where index=4 sans value, the PLAIN revert passes under both A and B. Must fix the oracle: anchor on value, or on an index in {5,6,7} \u2014 actually anchor on ALL FOUR (index,value) pairs, and ideally add a SECOND offset (e.g., +0x10) to kill the affine-escape family (two anchors pin base+stride... well, pin the map at two points; a crafted expression can still interpolate, e.g., piecewise, but each added anchor raises the cost). Honestly, no finite behavioral anchor set at aligned offsets can distinguish `>>1` from `(x>>3)<<2` since they AGREE on all aligned offsets \u2014 but that's fine, because (x>>3)<<2 is not a revert on the reachable set (identical behavior on all aligned stores; it differs only on the unreachable/put d set, where it's SAFER: max 108+3 = 111 in bounds \u2014 wait let me recompute: (0xdf>>3)<<2 = 27<<2 = 108, writes 108..111 \u2014 in bounds \u2713, and at aligned offsets identical to fix). So the affine escape that survives ALL aligned anchors is behaviorally IDENTICAL to the fix on every guest-reachable store AND bounds-safe on put d \u2014 i.e., it's not a revert at all; the oracle correctly doesn't care. Lovely \u2014 that closes the loop: under B with aligned-only anchors, any edit passing all anchors is either the fix's behavior on the reachable set or safer. The residual unanchored surface is exactly the put d/unaligned fringe, which B has decided (via the LINEAR disposition) to file rather than guard. Consistent.\n\nUnder A, by contrast, the un-anchored surface includes the guard itself, and its deletion is both natural and undetectable. So on Q4: B's hole exists in principle (single-anchor affine escapes) but every escape is either caught (plain revert) or non-behavioral on the reachable set; A's hole is a natural deletion that reopens the OOB. B is strictly better on oracle-durability. Plus the shared vacuity-at-index-4 defect must be fixed in the oracle itself regardless.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 one more consideration for Q4 under B: the comment. B ships \"the shift alone, with a comment\". If the detector row includes a source grep (e.g., for `>> 1` or for `re_tex[`), the comment could later satisfy a textual grep after a code revert (the comment mentioning the old/new token). Under B the comment is REQUIRED \u2014 so if the oracle has any textual component, its pattern must not be satisfiable from the comment. E.g., if the grep is for `>> 1` and B's comment says \"was: >> 3, now: >> 1\" \u2014 after a revert the code has `>> 3` but the comment still has `>> 1` \u2014 textual grep passes. Mitigation: write the comment without the literal token, or make the grep behavioral-only. Flag as a B-specific hazard IF the oracle greps source; not settleable which it greps from the packet (\"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 could be log lines like `re_tex[4] = ...` \u2014 I lean log-grep because \"stores 8 bytes ... and greps\" \u2014 the grep follows the store, suggesting it greps the store's OUTPUT. I'll present the log reading as primary and flag the source-grep reading.)\n\nOK. Also Q4 asks \"What is the smallest edit that reverts the shift and still passes...\" \u2014 under B, answer: the plain one-token revert FAILS a discriminating oracle (index 4 gets hw3 not hw0 / indices {1,2,3,4} vs {4,5,6,7}); the smallest PASSING revert-class edit is an affine one, e.g., `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3` (agrees with the fix at +0x08 \u2014 indices 4..7 with identical values \u2014 reverts at every other in-window offset; still in bounds), or if the oracle anchors only (5, hw1), the two-token `>> 3` + `tlbi += 4`. And note the overlap/vacuity issue. So: \"Does position B have an analogous hole?\" \u2014 Yes in the weak sense (anchor-specific affine escapes), No in the strong sense (no natural/lazy edit escapes, and no escape can reintroduce the OOB). A's hole is strictly worse: natural edit, safety-relevant, invisible.\n\nNow the VERDICT. Which position ships? I've argued: B, with the OOB instance FILED (extend the filing practice: document in the comment + file/extend the defect record), with the oracle fixed (anchor on value+index, avoid the 4-overlap, ideally two offsets), and with two named un-settleables recorded: (1) CRIME_RE_TEX's numeric value (must be \u2261 0 mod 256 for the & 0xff-masked >>1 to be correct at all \u2014 if not, the fix must subtract the base instead and premise 2's bounds analysis changes); (2) the completeness of non-put-d writers (DMA/other bus masters) \u2014 if any exists, the invariant B inherits is unsound and A's locality wins; (3) the licence's definition of \"addition\" (whether a conjunct on an existing condition is an addition); (4) whether sgilinearoob's trigger set is put d-only (the analogy's load-bearing element); (5) the detector row's delivery path (guest-aligned sd vs put d \u2014 if put d, it contradicts the file's own witnessing exclusion) and its grep target (log vs source; index-only vs index+value).\n\nWait \u2014 should the verdict be B, or should I push C-with-merge? Let me weigh once more. The brief's author moved A\u2192B and asks to be attacked. My honest audit: B is right, but for sharper reasons than \"precedent + licence\", and with the create/inherit point (Q2) conceded as a real cost that B must pay explicitly (file the new instance), not waved away. The author's inclination (\"creating a member of a filed class is acceptable\") \u2014 I'm asked to be hard on it. My ruling: creating is NOT the same as inheriting, and the difference is real \u2014 but it doesn't flip the verdict because the two remedies for \"created\" are (a) guard-at-birth (A) \u2014 which is un-anchorable under the project's own witnessing rules and contradicts the file's recorded disposition for the identical trigger set, and (b) don't-create (C) \u2014 which sacrifices the prerequisite and defers the indexing fix into the reader's round, at process cost and against a standing ruling. Given (a) and (b) both cost more than filing the created instance, B-with-filing is the least-bad. The honest formulation: B is not \"the shift is a correction so licence covers it and the OOB doesn't matter\"; B is \"the shift's benefit is real but deferred (no reader yet), its cost is a put d-only OOB instance; we pay the cost now (because the prerequisite ruling wants the store correct before the reader) and we record the debt the same way the file already records its sibling\". The create/inherit distinction is preserved by RECORDING the new instance as created-by-this-round with its own filing (not silently inheriting sgilinearoob's grandfathers status)... something like that.\n\nAlso Q1 asks: \"If B, say precisely why 'make the precondition local' is wrong, because I will reuse that reasoning elsewhere.\" So I need a crisp statement: Locality is wrong HERE because (1) the hazard the local guard defends against is unreachable by every path the model's own contract admits (guest instruction paths \u2014 premise 3) except a human tool whose behavior the project has already decided to treat as out-of-model (put d \u2014 LINEAR comment); a local guard therefore defends nothing in-model, and (2) whatever it defends out-of-model is unwitnessable by any in-model oracle, so the guard cannot be anchored \u2014 and unanchored guards rot (demonstrated escape); (3) locality is the right instinct when the invariant's SCOPE is unknown or evolving (unknown callers), and the packet genuinely cannot enumerate non-CPU callers \u2014 so the reusable principle is conditional: \"make preconditions local WHEN the guard defends against a path that is in-model or whose in-model status is unsettled; do not localize against paths the project has explicitly ruled out-of-model, and never add a guard no permitted oracle can witness.\" That's the reusable reasoning \u2014 and it includes its own escape hatch: if the DMA-caller question comes back \"yes, there is a non-CPU writer\", the locality argument reactivates and A wins. So the author can reuse it with the condition attached. \n\nHmm, but careful \u2014 is \"put d is out-of-model\" actually established? The LINEAR comment excludes put d as a WITNESS (repro methodology). It does NOT say \"put d behavior doesn't count\". A guard changes put d behavior (unaligned pokes get declined). Is changing put d behavior a delta the project cares about? The LINEAR precedent's disposition (filed, not guarded) implies: they accepted the put d-triggered over-run rather than alter behavior \u2014 which does implicitly treat put d's unaligned hazard as acceptable-and-recorded. So the precedent DOES contain a hazard-disposition, not just a repro rule. I'll note the two layers: witnessing rule (explicit) + disposition (filed-not-guarded, implicit in the outcome). B inherits BOTH; A's guard contradicts the second layer unless justified by create-vs-inherit. And I've handled create-vs-inherit above. Good.\n\nQ2 answer, crisp: Yes, creating is categorically different from inheriting \u2014 a filed defect class is a ledger of accepted debt, and \"accepted\" was decided per-instance (or per-class?) by a prior round with the then-current code; new instances are new decisions. The difference is real but it is a difference in DEFAULT, not in outcome-forcing: it means the creating round must EXPLICITLY re-affirm the filed disposition (name the new instance, state the trigger set, reference the class) rather than silently inherit. It does NOT mean the creating round must guard \u2014 because the reasons the class was filed-not-guarded (out-of-model trigger, empty guest delta, unwitnessable) apply with full force to the new instance. The inclination \"it is acceptable\" is convenient but also, in this case, correct \u2014 PROVIDED the acceptance is made explicit (the comment must say \"this fix introduces a put d-only over-run into re_linear_a at unaligned offsets; filed as <name>, same class/disposition as sgilinearoob\", not merely \"shift corrected\"). If the round ships B with only a \"shift is halfword index\" comment and NO filing of the new instance, then the convenience charge sticks: you created debt and didn't book it. So: acceptable to create only if booked at creation. That's the hard answer.\n\nQ3 answer: The row ships (B). C is rejected, but C's insight survives as a constraint: the fix has no in-model witness until sgirekills; therefore the detector row must be built now (guest-delivered aligned store + log grep on value\u2227index at a non-overlap index), and the sgirekills row should be recorded as the row that upgrades this from debug-print-witnessed to behaviorally-witnessed. The prerequisite tension dissolves under B: prerequisite satisfied, nothing deferred, nothing blocked. Also note: if the panel were to adopt C, it must simultaneously amend the sgirekills prerequisite (fold the shift into it) \u2014 otherwise the tension the brief names is real; but adopting C costs an amendment and leaves the mis-indexing to ship in the same diff as the reader, which is exactly the coupling the prerequisite ruling presumably wanted to avoid. Not settleable: the ruling's stated rationale. Fact: the text of the prerequisite ruling.\n\nQ4 answer: as analyzed. Headline: the oracle AS SPECIFIED may be vacuous (index 4 overlap) \u2014 that defect is shared and must be fixed first; then B's analogous hole exists only for crafted affine edits which are either caught or behavior-preserving/bounds-safe, while A's hole is a natural deletion that reopens the OOB. Also the B-comment/textual-grep hazard if the oracle greps source. And delivery path of the store (guest vs put d) \u2014 if put d, the oracle itself violates the file's witnessing rule; a guest-aligned sd at +0x08 is available and should be used. Wait \u2014 can a guest deliver a store at +0x08 in-window? The window is in CRIME register space; guest can sd to that physical address (mapped device). Premise 3 says guest sd is aligned \u2014 +0x08 is aligned \u2713. So yes, a guest-delivered detector is constructible. But hold on \u2014 the DEBUG print: \"a debug() on the next line\" \u2014 debug() in GXemul is verbose logging gated by debug_level; the detector row would enable it and grep the log. Fine.\n\nOne more check on Q4's premise: \"Under position A it is aligned, so deleting the alignment half of the guard escapes it \u2014 one seat already found that.\" Consistent with my analysis: the anchor +0x08 is aligned, so the alignment conjunct is un-witnessed by the anchor; deleting it doesn't change the anchored behavior. \u2713 READ/REASONED. And \"Does position B have an analogous hole?\" \u2014 my answer: no hole of equal severity; the analogous surface (the un-anchored remainder) is nonempty for any finite anchor set, but under B every escaping edit that survives all aligned anchors is provably identical to the fix on all aligned offsets (the (x>>3)<<2 family) \u2014 i.e., not a behavioral revert on the reachable set \u2014 or is caught. Under A the escaping edit is a revert of safety. Also note: under B the comment must not contain grep-satisfying text if the oracle greps source.\n\nNow, the CRIME_RE_TEX mod-256 point \u2014 let me make sure I'm right that the packet never states it. The packet: \"else if (relative_addr >= CRIME_RE_TEX && relative_addr < CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0)\" and \"int tlbi = ((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff;\" \u2014 the macro's value is never given. The agreed facts state indices 27..30 (>>3) and 111..114 (>>1 at 0xdf) \u2014 these are computed from `relative_addr & 0xff` == in-window offset, i.e., presupposing base \u2261 0 mod 256. Also possible: the agreed facts were computed by seats who DID read the tree (three positions, all agreed on premise 1) \u2014 likely the macro is 256-aligned (windows of 0xe0 with & 0xff masks are usually laid out at 256-blocks). But the packet explicitly says I should audit premises AS STATED and name un-settleables. This is THE un-settleable that everything hangs on. If CRIME_RE_TEX \u2262 0 (mod 256), then: (a) premise 2's \"bounds-safe under 8-alignment\" is false for >>1 (max (0xff)>>1 = 127 \u2192 writes up to 130 > 111 \u2014 OOB even aligned!), and (b) premise 1's fix as stated (one-token 3\u21921) is wrong; the correct fix is `(relative_addr - CRIME_RE_TEX) >> 1` (and the window check already guarantees offset < 0xe0, so bounds-safe under alignment: max (0xdf)>>1 = 111, writes \u2264 114 \u2014 the same unaligned fringe). So the one-token B-fix is only right under the mod-256 assumption; the robust fix subtracts the base \u2014 which is a LARGER diff than one token (position B's \"one-token correction\" claim depends on it). Name it: the single fact = the numeric value of the CRIME_RE_TEX macro (or equivalently, that `relative_addr & 0xff` equals the in-window offset for all in-window addresses).\n\nSimilarly the guard `(relative_addr & 7)` depends on base \u2261 0 mod 8 (weaker, surely true).\n\nAlso \u2014 subtle: even if base is 256-aligned, note the existing `& 0xff` mask makes the window 0..0xff addressable while the window check caps at 0xe0 \u2014 fine.\n\nLet me also double check premise 2's claim \"indices 27..30 at every in-window offset\" \u2014 at offset 0x00: 0>>3 = 0 \u2192 0..3. At 0xdf: 27..30. \"27..30 at every in-window offset\" \u2014 hmm, that reads as \"the max indices are 27..30\" or \"indices in {27..30} at the extreme offset\"? At offset 0, indices are 0..3, not 27..30. The sentence means: the WORST case is 27..30 (at 0xd8..0xdf). Slightly sloppy phrasing in the brief but the intent (bounds-safe unconditionally, worst case 27..30 < 112) is clear and correct. Not worth a paragraph \u2014 maybe one clause.\n\nAlso verify: is the loop 4 iterations? `for (size_t hwi = 0; hwi < len; hwi += sizeof(uint16_t))` with len==8: hwi = 0,2,4,6 \u2192 4 iterations, tlbi = base..base+3 \u2713. And `data[hwi]*256 + data[hwi+1]` \u2014 big-endian halfword assembly, consistent with len==8 gate (the decline of len != 8 protects data[] reads too... a len of, say, 3 would read data[2] beyond? \u2014 no, len!=8 is declined, so loop only runs for len==8 \u2713 \u2014 actually careful: the decline happens for len != 8 BEFORE the write arm, so the loop with hwi < len only executes when len == 8 \u2713).\n\nOne more: the brief says the read arm fatals (\"TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TEX\") \u2014 so a detector row must not read; only store + debug-grep. Consistent with my oracle design. Also means sgirekills (first reader) will have to implement the read arm \u2014 fine, out of scope.\n\nNow, what about position B's claim \"the shift is a one-token CORRECTION and the addition licence governs ADDITIONS\" \u2014 also note: under B, the diff is one token + comment. The comment itself is an addition \u2014 comments are presumably licence-exempt (not code). Fine.\n\nAnd Q1's demand: \"If B, say precisely why 'make the precondition local' is wrong, because I will reuse that reasoning elsewhere.\" \u2014 I'll give the conditional principle. Actually let me sharpen it into a sentence the author can reuse:\n\n\"Localize a precondition when the guard closes a path that is in-model or whose model status is unsettled; do not localize against a path the project has explicitly ruled out-of-model \u2014 and a guard is never justified if no permitted oracle can witness it, because unwitnessed guards are indistinguishable, to the project's own revert-detection process, from dead code.\"\n\nPlus the condition: \"This ruling is conditional on the caller enumeration being complete for in-model writers; if a non-CPU, non-debugger writer to this window exists (DMA), put d stops being the only exception and the guard becomes in-model \u2014 A then wins.\"\n\nGood. Also for Q2, the author says \"I am inclined to say it is acceptable, and that inclination is convenient for shipping.\" I should be hard: the test for whether convenience is driving the argument: would the argument survive if the created instance's trigger were in-model? No \u2014 so the argument's load-bearing element is precisely the out-of-model status of put d, which is a RECORDED judgment (the LINEAR comment + filed disposition), not a convenience. If that recorded judgment is wrong, both B and the LINEAR filing are wrong together \u2014 the create/inherit distinction doesn't rescue either. So the real question isn't create-vs-inherit; it's whether the put d exclusion is sound. And the packet shows the project has already answered that (explicitly for witnessing, implicitly for disposition). B doesn't NEED the create/inherit bridge to be crossed at all if one accepts the recorded judgment; the bridge is only needed for the consistency question \"why does TEX get filed-not-guarded for debt it just created?\" \u2014 answered: because the reasons for filing are trigger-based, not provenance-based. Hmm \u2014 but wait, then I've partially dissolved Q2: the distinction matters for BOOKKEEPING (new instance must be affirmatively filed, not silently inherited), not for DISPOSITION (guard vs file), because the disposition's rationale is trigger-based. That's a clean resolution: create-vs-inherit changes what paperwork you owe, not which remedy ships. I like that. It's also \"hard on\" the inclination in the right way: the inclination \"creating a member of a filed class is acceptable\" is acceptable ONLY as \"acceptable pending affirmative booking\"; it is NOT acceptable as \"the class is filed, so new members need no decision\". If the round ships B without naming the new instance in a filing, the convenience charge lands.\n\nBut I must also present the counter-position honestly: a hardening fork could adopt a stricter baseline: \"no NEW OOB writes, even out-of-model-triggered\". That baseline is coherent and would force A (guard at birth) or C (don't create). The packet doesn't state the fork's baseline charter. Is there an in-packet basis to choose? The fork is \"hardened\" and the panel's prior round chose filed-not-guarded for an existing OOB \u2014 which shows the fork tolerates recorded OOB debt at least for inherited cases. Does it tolerate created debt? Not settled by any in-packet fact; it's a charter question. What would settle it: the fork's charter/policy text on new-vs-existing defects (if such a policy exists). I'll name that. But pragmatically, three facts push to B: the prerequisite ruling (row should ship), the unwitnessability of the guard (A's guard can't be defended by the process), and the empty guest delta of the conjunct (its only delta is on the excluded path \u2014 and that delta is a BEHAVIOR CHANGE to put d, which is a real delta to a real tool: unaligned pokes would silently latch-decline instead of writing. Wait \u2014 is that bad? Under B, an unaligned put d poke overruns into re_linear_a silently (corruption). Under A, it's declined. From the debugger USER's perspective, A is friendlier (no silent corruption). But the LINEAR precedent already accepted silent corruption for the sibling arms \u2014 debugger users poking LINEAR unaligned corrupt something silently today, filed. Consistency: TEX joins its siblings. The debugger's own warning printf (\"address isn't aligned\") is the user's notice \u2014 arguably the RIGHT fix for the put d hazard is in the DEBUGGER (refuse unaligned put d, or make it a guard not a printf) \u2014 one fix covers BOTH the LINEAR filed defect and the new TEX instance! Oh, that's a good observation: the true root cause of the entire defect class is the debugger's non-guarding warning. A debugger-side guard (`if (addr & 7) { fprintf(...); return; }` in put d's 64-bit path) would close the whole class \u2014 sgilinearoob AND the new TEX instance \u2014 in one local, in-model (the debugger IS in-model as a tool) edit. But that's outside this row's scope (different file, dev debugger code) and would itself be an addition with a delta on a tool path... and would change behavior for the LINEAR arms' filed defect too (retrofitting the filed defect \u2014 which the prior round chose not to do). Still worth one sentence as the recorded alternative: if the panel wants the class closed, close it at the tap, not per-arm. It also shows A's per-arm guard is the wrong SHAPE even if you want guarding: it guards one arm of a class whose tap is shared. That strengthens B for THIS row and directs any guarding ambition to a separate row/round. I'll include that as a REASONED recommendation \u2014 file it as a follow-up row (debugger unaligned-put d guard), NOT shipped here (scope/licence).\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 careful: would a debugger-side guard be an \"addition\"? Yes, new code in another file, with a delta on the debugger path. Under the addition licence (whatever it says \u2014 not inlined), it'd need its own round. Fine as a recommendation.\n\nLet me also make sure I answer \"Q1: Which position ships?\" with a clean verdict line: \"VERDICT: Ship B \u2014 the shift plus comment, with the newly created put d-only over-run affirmatively filed as its own instance of the sgilinearoob class; reject A's guard (unwitnessable under the project's own rules, wrong shape for a tap-level class), reject C (contradicts the standing prerequisite ruling).\"\n\nWait, but should I double-check B vs \"B plus filing\" \u2014 is \"B plus filing\" still B? The positions: B = \"Ship the shift alone, with a comment. Do not ship the conjunct.\" A filing isn't code; it's process. B-with-a-filing is B plus paperwork. I'll say: ship B, and the comment must contain the booking (or the filing goes to the ledger). If the project's \"filed\" mechanism is a named defect ledger (sgilinearoob is named), then add sgitexoob or extend the entry. Fine.\n\nAlso should I reconsider: is there any chance the RIGHT answer is A? Let me steelman A once more, hard. A: `if (len != 8 || (relative_addr & 7))` decline+ latch. Defenses of A: (1) cheap; (2) local \u2014 robust to unknown callers (DMA); (3) prevents the created OOB \u2014 honors \"no new OOB in a hardening fork\"; (4) the decline already exists for len!=8, so the conjunct extends an existing local check \u2014 arguably not an \"addition\" at all but a widening of an existing correction... hmm, actually the existing `len != 8` check is PRE-EXISTING code (in the packet it's shown as existing). Modifying its condition from `len != 8` to `len != 8 || (relative_addr & 7)` is a one-conjunct edit to an existing line \u2014 is that an addition or a correction? Licence-dependent (not inlined). (5) The guard's put d delta (decline unaligned pokes) is arguably an improvement to the debugger UX (no silent corruption).\n\nAttacks on A (mine, from above): (i) contradicts the file's recorded disposition for the identical trigger class (consistency cost \u2014 future rounds will ask why TEX is guarded and LINEAR isn't; the answer \"because we created it\" requires the create/inherit bridge, which is exactly the contested Q2 \u2014 so A's consistency defense depends on winning Q2, which is unsettled); (ii) unwitnessable by any guest-legal oracle; witnessable only via put d, which the project's own comment rules out as laundering \u2014 so the guard is un-anchorable; the demonstrated escape (delete conjunct) shows it will rot; (iii) wrong shape: the class's tap is the debugger; per-arm guards multiply (there are presumably multiple arms in this class \u2014 LINEAR \u00d72 already filed); (iv) its guest delta is empty (agreed by B's statement, which A doesn't dispute \u2014 A's delta is entirely on the excluded path), so under the project's delta-accounting it's an addition with no in-model benefit \u2014 the definition of talisman code; (v) the DMA concern (unknown callers) \u2014 if real, it applies EQUALLY to the LINEAR arms today (they're unguarded) \u2014 so if DMA-unaligned were possible, the filed LINEAR defect would be guest/model-reachable TODAY and its filed status would be a live scandal; the prior round evidently satisfied itself that put d is the only exception (\"the ONE exception\") \u2014 the packet states this as the prior round's considered position. So the DMA worry is either already refuted in-tree (the prior round enumerated callers) or refutable by one fact. Under the packet, \"put d named as the ONE exception\" is stated \u2014 I take that as the prior round's caller enumeration being complete (READ: the author's summary of the comment). Hmm \u2014 \"put d named as the ONE exception\" \u2014 yes, the comment names it as the one exception, implying enumeration. So the DMA concern is likely already settled in-tree; still not verifiable by me, name it as the fact that would settle (any non-CPU writer to the window).\n\nGiven (i)\u2013(v), A loses. B ships. C loses on the prerequisite. OK, verdict is stable.\n\nNow \u2014 the response format:\n\n\"VERDICT LINE FIRST, so it survives truncation. Label each claim READ (from the packet) or REASONED. Never label anything MEASURED.\"\n\nSo the verdict line first. Then structured answers to Q1\u2013Q4, premise audit, and the not-settleable list with the single settling fact for each. Keep it tight \u2014 \"Do not pad.\" But there's a lot of substance; I'll aim for compact but complete. Use headers per question. Label claims READ/REASONED inline. Avoid restating the brief.\n\nLet me also recheck a couple of arithmetic claims I'll make:\n\n- Overlap at +0x08: >>3 gives base 1 \u2192 writes indices 1,2,3,4; >>1 gives base 4 \u2192 writes 4,5,6,7. Shared index: 4. \u2713\n- Value-at-4 differs: under >>3, re_tex[4] receives the FOURTH halfword (hwi=6: data[6],data[7]); under >>1, the FIRST (data[0],data[1]). \u2713 So grep must bind value.\n- `(x>>3)<<2 == x>>1` iff x \u2261 0 mod 8? Check x=8k: (8k>>3)<<2 = k<<2 = 4k; 8k>>1 = 4k \u2713. x = 8k+r, r>0: (x>>3)<<2 = 4k; x>>1 = 4k + r/2 \u2192 differ \u2713. So they agree exactly on the 8-aligned set. \u2713\n- `((x>>3)+3)` at x=8: 1+3 = 4 = 8>>1 \u2713; at 0x10: 2+3=5 vs 8 \u2717 revert \u2713; bounds: max 27+3 = 30, writes \u2264 33 < 112 \u2713.\n- `>>3` with stride 4 (`tlbi += 4`): at +0x08 writes 1,5,9,13; at 0x10: 2,6,10,14 \u2014 vs fix 8..11 \u2014 revert; bounds \u2264 27+12 = 39 \u2713. Escapes an oracle anchored on (index 5, hw1) \u2713.\n- Under >>1 with the & 0xff mask and non-256-aligned base: max index (0xff)>>1 = 127, writes \u2264 130 \u2265 112 \u2192 OOB even at aligned guest stores, if the mask misbehaves. \u2713 \u2014 this is the CRIME_RE_TEX-value dependency.\n\nAlso \u2014 premise 2 says \"invisible to ASAN\" \u2014 in-struct OOB, yes, ASAN doesn't pad intra-struct (unless -fsanitize=address with struct padding? ASAN detects intra-object overflow only with -fsanitize-address-use-after-scope? no \u2014 intra-object overflow detection is a separate flag/experimental; standard ASAN does NOT catch array-to-sibling-member overflow within the same struct). READ-consistent. Fine, no need to belabor.\n\nPremise 3: \"the unaligned ldl/ldr/sdl/sdr helper masks the address itself\" \u2014 so effective address becomes aligned; the store proceeds with len 8 at an ALIGNED address \u2192 under >>1, in bounds \u2713. Good \u2014 so ldl/sdr don't produce unaligned len-8. \u2713 (READ.)\n\nPremise 4: put d warning is printf, proceeds. \u2713 READ.\n\nPremise 5: no reader \u2192 \"observable severity today is nil\" \u2713 REASONED (modulo debug print; and modulo re_linear_a being corrupted \u2014 but that requires unaligned len-8, which can't happen today under >>3 at all (bounds-safe unconditionally). Right \u2014 TODAY (>>3), the OOB can't happen at all, even via put d. The over-run is created by the fix. \u2713 consistent with premise 2.)\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 let me double-check \">>3 bounds-safe unconditionally\" against put d unaligned: offset 0xdf \u2192 base 27, writes 27..30 \u2713. Yes, safe. So today there is NO OOB in the TEX arm by any path; the fix creates one on the put d path. And the LINEAR arms have an OOB today (via put d per the class description). So create-vs-inherit is exactly as framed. \u2713\n\nOne more Q3 consideration: the brief says \"a prior ruling made this row a PREREQUISITE of a HIGH row (sgirekills, which would create re_tex's first reader). 'Do not ship' and 'runs first' are in tension.\" Under B this resolves: the row ships, prerequisite met. But ALSO note: under B, sgirekills' reader will read re_tex \u2014 and the read arm currently FATALS (\"TODO: read\"). sgirekills must implement the read arm too. When the reader exists, the >>1 store indexing becomes load-bearing; the detector row for sgirekills will witness store+read round-trip. Fine \u2014 one sentence.\n\nAlso, the brief's framing \"This row is the natural anchored oracle\" for the +0x08 store \u2014 and asks the smallest revert that passes. I'll deliver the analysis with the two readings flagged, and the vacuity catch (index-4 overlap) as the headline defect of the oracle AS SPECIFIED \u2014 because \"greps the array name and index\" (no value mentioned). Actually hmm, \"greps the array name and index\" \u2014 if the grep is for `re_tex[4]` \u2014 under the FIX the log contains re_tex[4], re_tex[5], re_tex[6], re_tex[7]; under the BUG re_tex[1..4]. Grep `re_tex[4]` \u2192 present in both \u2192 vacuous \u2713. Grep `re_tex[5]` \u2192 only fix \u2192 discriminates, but then the \"smallest revert passing\" is the stride-4 edit (writes 5) \u2014 IF the value isn't checked; with value checked (hw1 at 5), stride-4 edit ALSO puts hw1 at 5 \u2713 \u2014 still passes! Wait: stride-4 edit writes re_tex[5] = hw1 (second halfword) \u2014 same as fix's re_tex[5] = hw1 \u2713\u2713. So even value-at-5 is matched by the stride-4 edit. And `((x>>3)+3)` writes 4..7 = fix exactly at the anchor \u2014 matches value at ANY of 4..7. So: any oracle anchored at +0x08 alone (any subset of the four (index,value) pairs) is passed by `((x>>3)+3)`. That's the clean statement: under B, the minimal escaping edit is `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3` \u2014 hmm, precedence: `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3 & 0xff` \u2014 careful writing. The original is `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`. The escape: `(((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3) & 0xff`. Diff: two tokens (+ 3 and parens). Or simpler: keep `& 0xff` outer. OK. And this edit passes ANY oracle built solely on +0x08 stores (all four indices+values identical) while reverting every other offset. Can it be caught? Only by anchoring a SECOND offset (e.g., +0x10: fix 8..11 vs escape 5..8 \u2014 overlap at 8 again! values differ though: fix puts hw0 at 8; escape puts hw3 at 8. With value-binding, +0x10 discriminates \u2713). So: two anchored offsets with value-binding kill `+3`; but `(x>>3)<<2` survives ALL aligned anchors (identical on all of them) \u2014 and is not a behavioral revert on the reachable set (and safer on the fringe). So the closure theorem for B: with value-bound anchors at \u22652 aligned offsets, every passing edit is either behaviorally-identical to the fix on all aligned offsets, or caught. And on the unaligned fringe, B has explicitly accepted filed risk \u2014 so oracle coverage = policy coverage. Beautiful and complete. Under A the corresponding closure FAILS for the guard: no anchor set can witness the conjunct (guest-legal anchors are aligned by premise 3; put d anchors are excluded by the file's own comment) \u2014 so the guard is unclosable, period. That's the precise asymmetry for Q4. \n\nNow assemble the answer. Structure:\n\n1. VERDICT line.\n2. Premise audit \u2014 the load-bearing un-settleable: CRIME_RE_TEX value (mod 256). Plus quick confirmations of the arithmetic-consistency of premises 1\u20132 (READ/REASONED).\n3. Q1: ships B; why \"local precondition\" is wrong (reusable conditional principle).\n4. Q2: create-vs-inherit \u2014 real but it changes BOOKKEEPING not DISPOSITION; the acceptable-with-booking ruling.\n5. Q3: row ships; C rejected; how the tension dissolves; what to record.\n6. Q4: oracle defects: (a) vacuity at index 4 (shared), (b) B's escaping edits (the +3 edit; the stride-4 edit) \u2014 exist but are either caught or non-reverts-on-reachable-set; (c) A's hole is strictly worse (natural deletion, reopens OOB, unclosable); (d) comment/textual-grep hazard; (e) delivery path of the store must be guest-aligned sd, not put d, or the oracle itself launders.\n7. Not-settleable list with single settling facts: (i) CRIME_RE_TEX numeric value; (ii) non-CPU writers/DMA caller enumeration; (iii) addition licence text re: conjuncts; (iv) sgilinearoob trigger set (put d-only?) / LINEAR arms' code; (v) prerequisite ruling's rationale text; (vi) detector row's grep target & delivery path (maybe fold into Q4); (vii) whether re_linear_a has readers (severity channel of the created OOB \u2014 worth including: if re_linear_a is live (used by LINEAR translation), the created corruption, though put d-only, corrupts a LIVE structure \u2014 whereas if re_linear_a is as dead as re_tex, the created OOB is literally writing into more dead state. This affects how much \"booking\" the filing needs. Single fact: readers of re_linear_a.)\n\nAlso the debugger-tap recommendation (one sentence, as follow-up row, not shipped).\n\nLength: substantial but tight. The instruction says \"Do not pad. A short answer that names one real defect beats a long one that restates the brief.\" I'll aim for something like 700\u20131000 words, dense, no restating. Maybe a bit more given four questions demanded answers. Let me draft.\n\nVerdict line: \"VERDICT: Ship B \u2014 the `>> 3` \u2192 `>> 1` shift plus comment \u2014 but only with the created over-run affirmatively booked (its own filed instance, not silent inheritance); reject A's conjunct (unwitnessable under the project's own oracle rules, and the wrong shape for a tap-level class), reject C (contradicts the standing prerequisite ruling). One premise everything hangs on is NOT in the packet: the numeric value of CRIME_RE_TEX (see P0).\"\n\nThen sections. Label READ/REASONED.\n\nP0 (premise audit): The index math in the agreed facts presupposes `(relative_addr & 0xff)` == in-window offset, i.e., CRIME_RE_TEX \u2261 0 (mod 256). READ: the code masks with `& 0xff` and the window is checked against the raw macro. If the macro is not 256-aligned, premise 2's \">>1 is bounds-safe under 8-alignment\" is FALSE \u2014 max `(0xff)>>1 = 127`, writes to 130, OOB even on guest-aligned stores \u2014 and the one-token fix is wrong; the correct edit is `(relative_addr - CRIME_RE_TEX) >> 1`. Since all three positions and all five agreed facts inherit this assumption, it is the packet's largest unhedged premise. Not settleable from packet; settling fact: the macro's value (or an in-packet statement that `relative_addr & 0xff` equals the in-window offset). Everything below is conditional on 256-alignment.\n\nAlso note (minor, READ-consistent): premise 2's \"indices 27..30 at every in-window offset\" is worst-case phrasing (at offset 0 the indices are 0..3); the intended claim \u2014 max index 30 < 112 under `>>3` \u2014 is arithmetically consistent. \u2713\n\nQ1 \u2014 B ships. Why \"make the precondition local\" is wrong, as a reusable principle:\n- REASONED: Locality earns its cost only when the guard closes a path that is in-model or of unsettled model status. Here the only path the conjunct closes is `put d`, which the file's own prior round explicitly ruled out-of-model for witnessing and, by filing-not-guarding the sibling arms, ruled acceptable-as-hazard (READ: the LINEAR precedent). A guard whose entire delta lies on a path the project has declared out-of-model is talisman code: its guest delta is empty (READ: B's statement, undisputed by A).\n- REASONED: Worse, the conjunct is UNANCHORABLE: guest-legal detector stores are aligned by premise 3 (READ), so no guest-delivered oracle can ever distinguish guarded from unguarded; put d could distinguish, but using it is what the file's own comment calls laundering (READ). A protection the permitted oracle set cannot witness is indistinguishable, to this process, from dead code \u2014 and one seat already demonstrated the rot: deleting the conjunct escapes the natural oracle (READ).\n- REASONED: Reusable form: \"Localize a precondition only against paths that are in-model or of unsettled status; never ship a guard no permitted oracle can witness.\" With its escape clause: if a non-CPU writer (DMA-class) can hit this window unaligned, put d stops being the one exception and A's locality wins \u2014 see P2 below.\n\nQ2 \u2014 the disanalogy is real, but it changes BOOKKEEPING, not DISPOSITION.\n- REASONED: Creating a member of a filed class differs from inheriting one: inherited debt carries a recorded decision; created debt needs a new decision at creation. A filed class is not a licence to manufacture members silently.\n- REASONED: But the disposition rationale at LINEAR is trigger-based, not provenance-based: out-of-model trigger (put d), empty guest delta, unwitnessable. Those properties are identical for the new TEX instance, so they yield the same disposition \u2014 file, don't guard.\n- So the inclination is acceptable ONLY in this form: \"create + affirmatively book at creation.\" If B ships with a mere `/* halfword index */` comment and no filing naming the new instance (trigger set: unaligned len-8 via put d only; victim: first halfwords of re_linear_a), the convenience charge lands \u2014 you manufactured debt off the books. The comment should state the created over-run explicitly and reference sgilinearoob as its class.\n- REASONED, and worth recording: the whole class's tap is the debugger's non-guarding printf (READ: premise 4). If the panel ever wants the class closed, the correct shape is one guard at the tap (`put d` refusing unaligned) \u2014 which would also retire sgilinearoob \u2014 not per-arm conjuncts multiplying across the file. That is a separate row under whatever addition licence governs; do not smuggle it into this one.\n\nQ3 \u2014 the row ships; C is rejected, and the tension dissolves under B.\n- READ: prior ruling made this row prerequisite to sgirekills (HIGH), which creates re_tex's first reader.\n- REASONED: C's \"any diff is strictly larger than doing nothing\" is true only under a present-day-severity metric; the prerequisite ruling already committed the panel to a sequencing metric: the store must be correct before the reader exists, or sgirekills ships a reader against a mis-indexed store and the mismatch gets baked into the pair. C relitigates that ruling without addressing it.\n- REASONED: Under B the tension dissolves outright: row ships, prerequisite satisfied, sgirekills unblocked, and its round upgrades this fix from debug-print-witnessed to behaviorally-witnessed (it must implement the read arm, which today fatals \u2014 READ).\n- REASONED: If the panel were to adopt C anyway, it would be obliged to amend the prerequisite (fold the shift into sgirekills) \u2014 an amendment whose cost exceeds the one-token diff it saves. Record C as rejected-on-the-standing-ruling.\n\nQ4 \u2014 the oracle has a defect BEFORE either position's hole matters.\n- READ+REASONED: At +0x08 the buggy `>>3` writes indices {1,2,3,4} and the fix writes {4,5,6,7} \u2014 they OVERLAP at index 4, with different halfwords in it (fourth vs first). A grep of \"array name and index\" that does not bind the VALUE is vacuous if the index is 4: the plain one-token revert passes it. Fix the oracle first: bind (index, value), and prefer anchoring a second offset (e.g., +0x10).\n- REASONED: B's analogous hole \u2014 yes, in a weak form. Given any oracle anchored solely at +0x08, the smallest escaping revert-class edit is `(((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) + 3)`: at +0x08 it produces indices 4..7 with the identical halfwords (passes even value-bound checks), while reverting every other in-window offset; it is still in bounds (max 33). The `>>3`-with-`tlbi += 4` edit similarly passes any anchor on (5, second halfword). So single-anchor oracles are always affine-escapable.\n- REASONED: But B's escapes close: with value-bound anchors at two aligned offsets, every passing edit is either caught or provably identical to the fix on ALL aligned offsets \u2014 the survivor family `(x>>3)<<2` agrees with `x>>1` at every 8-aligned offset and is bounds-safe even unaligned (max 111), i.e., it is not a behavioral revert on the reachable set. Oracle coverage then equals policy coverage (the unaligned fringe is exactly the risk B books).\n- REASONED: A's hole is strictly worse and unclosable: no guest-legal anchor set can witness the alignment conjunct at all (premise 3), so deleting it is invisible forever, and the deletion re-opens the actual OOB \u2014 a natural \"simplification\" edit, not an adversarial craft. Under B, the lazy revert (flip 1\u21923) is CAUGHT by a value-bound oracle; under A, the lazy deletion is NOT.\n- REASONED (flag): two oracle specs are unstated in the packet. (a) How the row delivers the store: it must be a guest-aligned `sd`, not `put d` \u2014 a put d-delivered detector row violates the file's own witnessing rule (READ) and would be self-laundering. (b) What the grep reads: if it greps SOURCE text for the index token, then B's required comment becomes the escape hatch \u2014 never write the literal grep target into the comment; a reverting editor leaves the comment satisfying the grep. Prefer log-grep on (index, value).\n\nThen the not-settleable ledger:\n\nP0: CRIME_RE_TEX value (must be \u22610 mod 256 for the packet's entire index/bounds analysis and the one-token fix). Settling fact: the macro's numeric value.\nP1: whether re_linear_a has readers \u2014 determines whether the created over-run corrupts a live structure or more dead state; shapes the filing's severity line, not the disposition. Settling fact: any read of re_linear_a in the model.\nP2: completeness of the writer enumeration \u2014 \"put d is the ONE exception\" is the prior round's claim (READ as reported), but non-CPU writers (DMA-class callers of memory_rw into this window) are outside premises 3\u20134; if one exists unaligned, A's locality reactivates. Settling fact: enumeration of non-CPU callers that can target this window with len 8.\nP3: whether the conjunct-on-existing-condition is an \"addition\" under the licence \u2014 B's licence argument is uncheckable from the packet. Settling fact: the licence text's definition of addition (token-level vs statement-level). Note: my case for B does not stand on the licence; it stands on unwitnessability + precedent + shape. So P3 is B's argument's weak limb; drop it or verify it.\nP4: whether sgilinearoob's trigger set is put d-only (the \"exact class\" claim). Settling fact: the LINEAR arms' index math + window, or the filing's trigger analysis. If LINEAR's over-run were guest-reachable, the precedent inverts (it would be a scandal, not a precedent) and the panel should reopen it \u2014 but that is a different emergency, not a reason to guard TEX.\nP5: the prerequisite ruling's rationale (whether folding the shift into sgirekills is permitted) \u2014 moot if B ships, but record it. Actually \u2014 since I ship B, P5 is not needed. Keep it as one line or drop. I'll keep one line only if it earns its place. Maybe drop P5 to reduce padding, mention in Q3 that under B it's moot.\n\nAlso small: should I note that premise 2's \"invisible to ASAN\" is right (intra-struct overflow isn't caught by default ASAN)? One clause, REASONED. Actually the packet asserts it as agreed fact; fine, no need to dispute; skip or one clause.\n\nOne more check on my Q4 claim: \"at +0x08 the `+3` escape gives base (8>>3)+3 = 4, writes 4,5,6,7 with hw0..hw3 \u2014 identical trace to fix.\" \u2713. And at +0x10: (16>>3)+3 = 5 \u2192 writes 5..8; fix writes 8..11. Overlap at 8, values differ (escape puts hw3 at 8; fix puts hw0 at 8) \u2192 value-bound second anchor catches \u2713.\n\nAnd the stride-4 escape at +0x10: base 2 \u2192 2,6,10,14 vs fix 8..11: no overlap with {8,9,10,11}? 10 is in both! escape writes hw2 at 10; fix writes hw2 at 10! (fix: 8=hw0,9=hw1,10=hw2,11=hw3; escape: 2=hw0,6=hw1,10=hw2,14=hw3.) So at +0x10, (10,hw2) matches! Ugh \u2014 so if the second anchor greps (10,hw2), the stride-4 escape passes BOTH anchors. If it greps (8,hw0) or (11,hw3) or (9,hw1), it fails. So \"two value-bound anchors suffice\" \u2014 need the right pairs. With (index,value) bound at two offsets choosing non-colliding pairs, the affine family (base = (x>>3)+c, stride variants) has few parameters; two well-chosen anchors pin it. The `+3` escape at +0x10 writes 5..8; fix 8..11; overlap at 8 with DIFFERENT values (escape hw3 at 8, fix hw0 at 8) \u2192 any value-bound anchor at +0x10 catches +3 \u2713. The stride-4 escape at +0x10 is caught by anchoring (8,hw0) \u2713. Can any single edit survive two arbitrary value-bound anchors? The edit has ~2 degrees of freedom (function of x composed with loop); anchors at two offsets give 8 (index,value) constraints each... a determined adversary can write a piecewise/conditional expression \u2014 no finite behavioral anchor set is fully escape-proof against ADVERSARIAL edits (you can always special-case the anchored inputs: `tlbi = (relative_addr == ANCHOR1) ? 4 : ((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3);`). True! The strongest honest statement: behavioral oracles anchor against NATURAL reverts, not adversarial ones; an oracle that special-case-proof is impossible. So the criterion: does the oracle catch the LAZY revert and the PLAUSIBLE refactor? Under B with value-bound multi-offset anchors: yes. Under A: the conjunct deletion is lazy AND plausible AND invisible \u2014 the worst case. And the special-case escape applies to A equally. So my comparative claim survives; I'll state the adversarial limit honestly in one clause.\n\nAlso note under A, is there any way to catch conjunct-deletion? Source grep for `& 7` \u2014 textual, satiable by the comment; or a put d-based detector row \u2014 excluded as laundering by the file's own comment... wait, is a put d-based DETECTOR row actually excluded? The comment says put d must not be used to WITNESS the over-run \u2014 i.e., to demonstrate/repro the OOB. Using put d to witness the GUARD's DECLINE is witnessing a prevention, not an over-run... Hmm. The comment's rationale: put d \"bypasses the guest instruction path, so a reproduction built on it would be laundering rather than witnessing\" \u2014 the rationale is about repros of guest-visible behavior. A guard against put d itself can ONLY be tested via put d. Is that laundering? The guard's entire delta IS on the put d path, so testing it via put d isn't laundering guest behavior \u2014 there's no guest behavior to launder; it's directly testing the tool-path contract. Hmm \u2014 so A's guard IS witnessable, via a put d-based detector row that asserts the decline (store does not land / latch records decline). That contradicts my \"unclosable\" claim! Let me re-think.\n\nThe LINEAR comment's exclusion is about witnessing the OVER-RUN (the defect) via put d \u2014 because a defect repro must run through the model's real interface (guest instructions) to count as witnessing. For a guard whose only delta is on the debugger path, the \"real interface\" IS the debugger. So a detector row: `put d <unaligned addr>` then assert re_tex unchanged (grep log for absence / assert the latch) \u2014 that witnesses the guard without laundering anything. It IS anchorable \u2014 but at the price of adding put d-based detector rows to the suite, which the project's recorded methodology has so far avoided (and the demonstrated A-hole was found against the guest-anchored oracle; a put d-based oracle row would close it). Hmm \u2014 so the honest statement: A's conjunct is witnessable ONLY by admitting put d into the detector methodology, which the project has so far refused; whether that refusal extends from \"witnessing over-runs\" to \"witnessing guards on the debugger path\" is a METHODOLOGY question the packet does not settle. It's a judgment: the comment's stated rationale (bypasses guest path \u2192 laundering) does NOT apply to testing the debugger's own contract, so extending the methodology is defensible; but B avoids needing the extension at all. So the ranking survives: B needs no methodology extension; A needs one (plus the create/inherit bridge, plus the consistency cost). I'll state this fairly \u2014 it strengthens the answer's credibility, and it's the kind of \"only works if you already believe the author\" catch the packet asked me to hunt. Actually \u2014 wait, does the same reasoning apply to the OOB itself? Under B, could a detector row witness the FILED over-run via put d? The comment says NO \u2014 put d repros of the over-run are laundering. So under B, the created OOB stays unwitnessed (filed, documented) \u2014 consistent with sgilinearoob's status (filed, unwitnessed). Symmetry preserved \u2713. Under A with put d-based rows admitted, you'd witness the guard but still not the (now-prevented) over-run. Fine.\n\nLet me also double-check the claimed \"put d must be the delivery for unaligned len-8\" \u2014 premise 4 says put d CAN deliver it; premise 3 says guest cannot. Any other debugger commands? \"put\" with other widths \u2014 put d is 64-bit. Fine.\n\nI think I should also briefly note the `& 0xff` trailing mask is inert under the fix (max index 111 < 256) so the one-token change leaves no latent mask hazard \u2014 one clause, REASONED, conditional on P0.\n\nOkay \u2014 also the instruction: \"Label each claim READ (from the packet) or REASONED.\" I'll label inline like (READ) / (REASONED). And \"Never label anything MEASURED.\"\n\nFormat: verdict line first, then compact sections. Headers minimal. Given the four questions and the premise audit, ~900-1100 words is justified; keep sentences tight. No restating the brief beyond what's needed for reference. Use the labels. Let me write it.\n\nOne more consideration on the verdict line: it must survive truncation and carry the essentials: B ships with booking; A rejected (unwitnessable/wrong shape); C rejected (prerequisite); P0 unverified premise (CRIME_RE_TEX mod 256). Good.\n\nAlso \u2014 the author asked in Q1 \"If B, say precisely why 'make the precondition local' is wrong, because I will reuse that reasoning elsewhere.\" My reusable principle should be quotable. Let me finalize it:\n\n\"Guards are bought for paths, not for code. A local guard pays only when it closes a path that is in-model or of unsettled model status; a guard whose entire delta lies on a path the project has ruled out-of-model buys nothing in-model, and if no permitted oracle can witness it, it is indistinguishable from dead code and will be deleted as such.\"\n\nWith the condition: \"If the writer enumeration for this window is incomplete (non-CPU bus masters), put d is not the one exception and the guard becomes in-model \u2014 then A wins. The packet cannot settle that enumeration; the prior round's comment claims put d is the ONE exception (READ), and I flag the residual.\"\n\nGood. Also note that under B, the conjunct's absence is ALSO consistent with the \"decline, latched\" pattern only for len != 8 \u2014 i.e., B leaves unaligned len-8 stores WRITING (loop runs). Fine.\n\nAlso, should the comment under B mention the decline semantics? No, keep scope.\n\nWrite it now. Also remember: \"Do not attempt file reading and do not announce plans to.\" I won't.\n\nWord budget: I", "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 360521394398}
